export default [{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":1,"text":"Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess A research report on the ancient Indian game of the \"four limbs,\" its stories, its oldest surviving pieces, and its long journey to modern chess How to read this report This report follows one idea across about fifteen centuries: a battlefield turned into a board. It begins with the Sanskrit word chaturanga and the army it described, moves through the earliest written and archaeological evidence, retells the legends people told about the game (including the ones connected with the Mahabharata and Ramayana), explains how the game was played and why, and then follows it through Persia, the Arab world, East Asia and Europe until it becomes the chess played today. One rule runs through the whole report: separate what is documented from what is legend. Chess has more beautiful origin stories than almost any other human invention, and most of them are not history. Where a story is a legend, a later invention or a modern novel, the text says so. Where scholars disagree, both sides are given. The main scholarly foundation is H. J. R. Murray's A History of Chess (1913), later refined by Henry Davidson, Harry Golombek, Richard Eales and others, together with Persian, Arabic and Sanskrit sources and"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":1,"text":"n is H. J. R. Murray's A History of Chess (1913), later refined by Henry Davidson, Harry Golombek, Richard Eales and others, together with Persian, Arabic and Sanskrit sources and archaeological finds from Central Asia. Sources are listed at the end. Summary in one page Name. Chaturanga (Sanskrit caturaṅ ga) means \"having four limbs\" or \"four parts.\" In epic poetry it commonly means \"army.\" The four parts were elephants, chariots, cavalry and infantry. Where and when. The prevailing view is that the game arose in northern India in or before the early seventh century CE, in the later Gupta period (the sixth century is the usual estimate). The first clear mention of the name in connection with the game comes from Banabhatta's Harshacharita, written around 625 CE. What it was. A two-player war game on an 8×8 uncolored board called the ashtapada. Each side had a king (raja), a counsellor (mantri), two elephants (gaja), two horses (ashva), two chariots (ratha) and eight foot soldiers (padati). The exact rules were Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 1 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":2,"text":"never recorded in a surviving Indian manual from that early period, so they are reconstructed from its descendants. How it spread. To Sasanian Persia (where it became chatrang, around the reign of Khosrow I, 531–579), then to the Arab world (shatranj), then through Muslim Spain and Italy into Europe. Eastwards it became the Southeast Asian games (Burmese sittuyin, Thai makruk, Cambodian ouk chatrang), and probably the Chinese xiangqi, Korean janggi and Japanese shogi. Oldest pieces. The oldest securely identified chess pieces are the seven ivory figures from Afrasiab (ancient Samarkand, Uzbekistan), dated to roughly the seventh to eighth century. Older figures from Dalverzin-tepe are debated. Modern chess. The powerful queen and bishop appeared in Spain and Italy around 1475 (the poem Scachs d'amor, Valencia). The modern tournament world began with London 1851, the Staunton pieces (1849) and FIDE (1924). Legends. The Mahabharata contains a famous dice game but not chess. Chess legends attach to Mandodari (wife of Ravana), to the Persian sage Bozorgmehr, to the Indian wise man Sissa and to Shiva and Parvati. None is proven history. Timeline at a glance Period Event Status c. 1500–500 BCE Dice and board games exist in India; the"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":2,"text":"r, to the Indian wise man Sissa and to Shiva and Parvati. None is proven history. Timeline at a glance Period Event Status c. 1500–500 BCE Dice and board games exist in India; the word chaturanga is used for armies Documented (the word), not chess c. 5th century BCE onward Buddhist texts list board games on 8 and 10 rows (ashtapada, dasapada) that monks should avoid Documented, but these are not clearly chess c. 300–500 CE Mahabharata reaches its final form; it describes the four-part army and the dice game Documented c. 5th century CE Chinese pilgrim Fa Xian travels India and does not mention chess Argument from silence c. 600 CE Subandhu's Vasavadatta uses a chessboard image (frogs on black squares) Documented, interpretation debated Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 2 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":3,"text":"Period Event Status c. 600–625 CE Harshacharita by Banabhatta names chaturanga alongside the ashtapada Earliest clear Sanskrit mention 6th–7th century Pahlavi (Middle Persian) texts describe chatrang arriving from India Documented, with legendary content c. 7th–8th century Afrasiab ivory set, Samarkand Oldest securely identified pieces 8th–9th century Abstract pieces at Nishapur; Arabic writers on shatranj Documented c. 1000 Ferdowsi's Shahnameh; al-Biruni describes four-handed chaturaji Documented 11th–12th century Chess spreads across Europe; the Charlemagne and Lewis pieces are carved Documented c. 1150 Manasollasa in India describes chaturanga among royal pastimes Documented c. 1475 Queen and bishop gain modern powers (Scachs d'amor, Valencia) Documented 1849–1851 Staunton pieces; first international tournament in London Documented 1886 First official world champion, Wilhelm Steinitz Documented 1924 FIDE founded in Paris Documented 1988 Viswanathan Anand becomes India's first grandmaster Documented Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 3 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":4,"text":"Chapter 1. The word and the idea: \"four limbs\" 1.1 What chaturanga means Chaturanga is a Sanskrit compound. Chatur means \"four\" and anga means \"limb,\" \"part\" or \"member.\" Grammarians call a compound of this type a bahuvrihi: it names a thing by describing what it possesses. A chaturanga is therefore \"that which has four limbs.\" In the ancient Sanskrit epics the word is used again and again for an army with four arms: 1. Elephants (gaja, hasti), the armored heavy units, the tanks of the ancient world; 2. Chariots (ratha), the mobile platforms for the great warriors and their archers; 3. Cavalry (ashva, horse), the fast scouts and flankers; 4. Infantry (padati, pada, bhata), the foot soldiers who made up the bulk of any host. This fourfold division of military strength was an established way of thinking in Indian statecraft long before any board game used it. When a poet in the epics wrote that a king marched out with a chaturanga force, the audience understood an organized army with all four branches present. 1.2 The army in the Mahabharata The Mahabharata gives the fourfold army a precise mathematical structure. Its basic unit is the akshauhini, a full army division. According to the epic's counting scheme, an akshauhini is made"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":4,"text":"ta gives the fourfold army a precise mathematical structure. Its basic unit is the akshauhini, a full army division. According to the epic's counting scheme, an akshauhini is made up of: 21,870 chariots, 21,870 elephants, 65,610 horses, and 109,350 foot soldiers. Notice the ratios. For every chariot there is one elephant, three horses and five foot soldiers. The Kurukshetra war in the epic is said to involve eighteen akshauhinis in total, eleven on the Kaurava side and seven on the Pandava side. Whatever one thinks of the figures (they are literary, not census numbers), they show how systematically the epic thinks about armies as four-part structures with fixed proportions. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 4 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":5,"text":"Some chess historians have pointed out that the akshauhini's arrangement is reminiscent of the starting setup of chaturanga, with chariots, elephants and horses represented in pairs and infantry in the front rank. The comparison is suggestive, but it should be treated with care: the epic describes battle arrays, not board games, and the resemblance may be partly the result of the game having been designed to imitate the army as Indians understood it. 1.3 From army to game The key conceptual step is simple but enormously important. Somebody, at some point, realized that a war could be modeled on a grid. Each unit type could be given a distinct way of moving that expressed its character: The elephant is heavy, powerful, hard to stop. The horse leaps over obstacles and strikes from unexpected angles. The chariot charges in straight lines at speed. The foot soldier advances slowly, one step at a time, and captures only what is in front of him diagonally, since spears and swords reach forward. The king, who must not fall, moves one careful step at a time. The counsellor (mantri, minister) stays close to the king and moves only a short distance. The winning condition was equally logical. A real battle was lost when the king was"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":5,"text":"ime. The counsellor (mantri, minister) stays close to the king and moves only a short distance. The winning condition was equally logical. A real battle was lost when the king was captured, so the game was decided by the king's fate. That one decision, to make victory depend on a single piece, is what makes chaturanga the ancestor of all later chess. Encyclopaedia Britannica singles out exactly these features: pieces with different powers, and a win decided by the fate of one piece. That is why chess, even today, is described as a war game, with its \"attacks,\" \"defenses,\" \"sacrifices\" and \"campaigns.\" The metaphor is not a modern flourish. It is the founding idea. 1.4 How the name travelled The Sanskrit word did not stay in India. It was borrowed and reshaped by every culture that received the game: Persian (Middle Persian): chatrang. The vowels were worn down, a natural process when a foreign word is borrowed. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 5 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":6,"text":"Arabic: shatranj (the Arabs have no \"ch\" sound in this position and no \"ng\" cluster at the end, so the form changed again). Spanish and Portuguese: ajedrez, xadrez, from Arabic ash-shatranj. Greek: zatrikion, taken from Persian chatrang. Tibetan: chandaraki, plainly derived from chaturanga. Burmese: sittuyin. In Burmese sit means \"army,\" so the name keeps the sense of the original. Malay/Indonesian: catur, from chatur. Thai: makruk (a different word, but the game itself belongs to the same family). Cambodian: ouk chatrang, again plainly from chaturanga. The survival of the Sanskrit root across so many languages is itself one of the strongest pieces of evidence for the Indian origin of the game. When a name travels with a game, it usually tells you where the game came from. Chapter 2. Before chess: boards, dice and the ashtapada 2.1 The ashtapada board Ancient Indian literature mentions a board called the ashtapada, which in Sanskrit means \"eight-footed\" or \"eight-stepped.\" It is a square board of 8×8 squares, 64 in all, and in Indian practice it was not colored in alternating light and dark squares. This is a detail that surprises modern players: the checkered pattern of today's boards came much later. Early chaturanga boards"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":6,"text":"not colored in alternating light and dark squares. This is a detail that surprises modern players: the checkered pattern of today's boards came much later. Early chaturanga boards were plain. Some ashtapada boards carry special markings on certain squares. The purpose of these marks is not known. Murray suggested that the board may also have been used for a race game played with dice, in which such marks had a function, and that the marks were simply kept on the board by tradition when the new war game borrowed it. Others note that the marked squares coincide with positions that certain pieces could never reach under the reconstructed movement rules, which supports the idea that the marks belonged to some earlier use. The important point is that the board came first. A grid of 64 squares existed as a gaming surface before chess did. When the army game was invented, its designers placed it on the familiar ashtapada, and the game took on the name chaturanga, the \"army game.\" One Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 6 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":7,"text":"careful chess historian summarizes it this way: when chess was put on the ashtapada, it was natural to call it chaturanga. 2.2 Dice games in ancient India India had a very old and very powerful tradition of dice. Dice appear in the Rigveda, and gambling with dice is one of the most dramatic episodes in the Mahabharata (see Chapter 4). A whole class of Indian board games, the race games, combined board and dice. The familiar pachisi (the ancestor of Ludo and Parcheesi) and chowka bhara belong to this tradition. This matters for chess history because several early theories assumed that chaturanga was itself a dice game. We will return to this under the Cox–Forbes theory below. The mainstream view today is the opposite: two-player chaturanga, as the ancestor of chess, was a game of pure skill, and the dice-driven four-player variant (chaturaji) is a later offshoot. 2.3 Buddhist lists of games Early Buddhist texts, such as the Brahmajala Sutta and the monastic rules (the Vinaya), contain lists of entertainments and games that monks should not indulge in. These lists include board games played on boards of eight rows (ashtapada) and ten rows (dasapada), as well as games played on an imaginary board in the air. Some earlier scholars,"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":7,"text":"hese lists include board games played on boards of eight rows (ashtapada) and ten rows (dasapada), as well as games played on an imaginary board in the air. Some earlier scholars, including Murray in his first treatment, tried to read these passages as evidence for early chess. Murray later became unsure about that interpretation, and most specialists today treat the passages as evidence for board games on 8×8 and 10×10 grids, not chess in particular. Still, the lists are valuable. They show that gridded game boards were common and socially noticed in the centuries around the time of the Buddha, and that religious teachers saw such games as distractions. That attitude toward board games, as time- wasting or morally risky, would follow chess for the next two thousand years. 2.4 The Cox–Forbes theory In the late eighteenth century a British officer in Burma, Hiram Cox, came across the four- handed dice game later called chaturaji (\"four kings\"). In the nineteenth century Duncan Forbes developed the idea into the Cox–Forbes theory: that four-handed, dice-driven Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 7 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":8,"text":"chaturaji was the original form of the game, and that two-player chess was a later simplification created by removing the dice and merging the allied players. The theory had an attractive logic. The name chaturanga means \"four limbs,\" and a game with four players, each with an army, seemed to match it nicely. A tradition (reported in medieval and later sources) also held that the dice were removed on religious grounds, since gambling was forbidden. Murray's 1913 study dismantled it. His main arguments were: 1. The earliest detailed description of the four-handed game is by al-Biruni around 1030, centuries after the first mentions of the two-player game in Persian and Sanskrit. 2. The Persian texts about chess describe a two-player game with no dice. 3. Words such as chaturanga were used for \"army\" long before any game, so the name does not by itself imply four players. Today most scholars regard chaturaji as a derivative of chaturanga, an adaptation that added dice and extra players. Still, the Cox–Forbes theory is historically important: it shows how seriously nineteenth-century scholars took the Indian roots of the game, and it explains why you will still find it in older books and popular articles. 2.5 The Lothal claim A"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":8,"text":"how seriously nineteenth-century scholars took the Indian roots of the game, and it explains why you will still find it in older books and popular articles. 2.5 The Lothal claim A further claim sometimes appears in popular Indian writing: that archaeologists found chaturanga pieces at the Indus Valley site of Lothal, dated by radiocarbon to about 2500 BCE, proving that the game is 4,500 years old. This claim has been challenged by chess historians. The objections are that no proof links the objects to chess, that the 64-square board in early India belonged to a dice race game, and that no written evidence for chess exists before about the sixth century CE. In the debate over the claim, one participant described it as reviving the old Cox–Forbes theory, which Murray had already shown to be unsupported. The responsible summary is this: the Indus civilization certainly had games, dice and gaming pieces, but there is no accepted evidence that it had chess. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 8 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":9,"text":"Chapter 3. The first written evidence 3.1 An argument from silence Historians of chess have long noticed how quietly the game enters history. Murray and Richard Eales concluded that before the seventh century, the existence of chess in any land cannot be demonstrated. One often-cited observation is that the Chinese Buddhist pilgrim Fa Xian, who travelled widely through India at the beginning of the fifth century and wrote a detailed account, never mentions chess. An argument from silence is never conclusive, but taken with the lack of early references it suggests that the game did not yet exist, or was not yet widely known, around 400 CE. Then, within a few decades around the year 600, references suddenly appear in several languages. That cluster is why scholars place the invention around the sixth century. 3.2 Subandhu's frogs (c. 600) The Sanskrit prose romance Vasavadatta, by Subandhu, describes the rainy season in a playful image: the season plays a game, with frogs as the playing pieces, yellow and green in color, as though stained with lac, leaping onto the black squares of the board. The passage appears to describe a chessboard-like game with two-colored pieces. Some scholars read it as an early literary allusion to chess;"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":9,"text":"onto the black squares of the board. The passage appears to describe a chessboard-like game with two-colored pieces. Some scholars read it as an early literary allusion to chess; the poem does not say the word chaturanga, so it is not conclusive. 3.3 Banabhatta's Harshacharita (c. 625) The most important Sanskrit reference is in the Harshacharita, the biography of the emperor Harsha (who ruled from 606 to 647) by his court poet Banabhatta. In a passage praising the king's peaceful rule, the poet uses a series of puns: under this king only bees quarrel (over the dew), the only feet that are cut off are the feet of verse (in prosody), and it is only on the ashtapada that one learns how to draw up a chaturanga, since no one's four limbs are cut off in real punishment. There are two things to understand here. First, the pun on chaturanga works because the word has two meanings: the four-part army, and the game. Second, the same line mentions the ashtapada, the board, so the poet associates the game directly with its 64- square board. This is generally accepted as the earliest clear reference to the name Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 9 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":10,"text":"chaturanga in connection with the game. Scholars note that the passage also gives the first known use of the idea that the game teaches the arrangement of an army without the cruelty of war, a theme that would be repeated for centuries. 3.4 The Persian witnesses Three Middle Persian (Pahlavi) texts mention chatrang: 1. The Karnamak-i Ardashir-i Papakan, a legendary account of Ardashir I, founder of the Sasanian dynasty (r. 224–241), which lists chess among his accomplishments. Because the work is full of legends and was written much later than Ardashir's lifetime, it proves only that chess was popular when the text was composed. 2. Khusraw ud Redag (\"Khosrow and the Page\"), in which a boy boasts of his skill in chess, backgammon and other arts. This text also seems to be late. 3. Wizarishn i Chatrang (\"The Explanation of Chess\"), also known as the Chatrang- namak, which tells the most famous story of how chess arrived in Persia. It is treated in detail in Chapter 8. The Pahlavi texts do not date themselves, and the first two may include post-Sasanian editing, so they cannot independently prove an early date. The Wizarishn is the key text, and its date (around the early seventh century) is consistent with the Sanskrit evidence."},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":10,"text":"editing, so they cannot independently prove an early date. The Wizarishn is the key text, and its date (around the early seventh century) is consistent with the Sanskrit evidence. 3.5 What the earliest sources agree on Putting the earliest sources side by side, certain points are consistent: The game is Indian in name, because every early Persian and Arabic name is derived from the Sanskrit. The game is military in character, a model of an army. It was a courtly, aristocratic pastime from the beginning, associated with kings, ministers and ambassadors. It was already so sophisticated that it served as a test of wisdom between kingdoms, as the story of the Indian envoy at Khosrow's court shows. What the earliest sources do not give is a complete rulebook. Murray observed that because Sanskrit writing on chaturanga is so sparse, the original game may be lost to history, and that knowledge of it comes second-hand, mostly from Muslim sources describing shatranj. This is an essential caution for the next chapters. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 10 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":11,"text":"Chapter 4. Stories of chess: the Mahabharata, the Ramayana and the legends The first thing a student must know about \"chess in the Mahabharata\" is that the question has a surprising answer. The Mahabharata does not describe a game of chess being played. What it contains is something else: the most famous gambling scene in world literature, a single passage in which a board-and-dice game is mentioned, and an enormous amount of thinking about armies and battle formations. Together these made the soil in which chaturanga grew. This chapter separates the pieces. 4.1 The great dice game (Sabha Parva) In the Sabha Parva, the book of the assembly hall, the Kaurava prince Duryodhana returns from the Pandavas' royal consecration (the Rajasuya sacrifice) consumed by jealousy of his cousin Yudhishthira. His uncle Shakuni, the prince of Gandhara and the brother of Gandhari, is a master of dice and, according to the tradition, a cheat. Shakuni proposes a game of dice. Yudhishthira, bound by his code of honor not to refuse a challenge, accepts. What follows is the turning point of the whole epic. Yudhishthira loses his wealth, his kingdom, his brothers and finally himself. He then stakes and loses Draupadi, the common wife of the five"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":11,"text":"s the turning point of the whole epic. Yudhishthira loses his wealth, his kingdom, his brothers and finally himself. He then stakes and loses Draupadi, the common wife of the five Pandavas, who is dragged into the hall and humiliated. The result of the game is the exile of the Pandavas for thirteen years, and the grievance that leads to the Kurukshetra war. Two points are worth making for chess history: 1. This was a game of chance, not skill. The tragedy of the scene is that Yudhishthira has no way to out-think a loaded set of dice. 2. The episode helps explain why, for centuries afterwards, Indian moral and religious writers warned against gambling, and why a skill-only game like chaturanga could later be presented as a respectable alternative. The relief carving of Shakuni and Yudhishthira playing dice at Lepakshi (in Andhra Pradesh) is one of the best-known images of this scene in Indian art. It shows a game, but not chess. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 11 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":12,"text":"4.2 Yudhishthira as Kanka (Virata Parva) The passage that has made some readers think of chess comes in Book 4, the Virata Parva. During the thirteenth year of exile the Pandavas must live in disguise. Yudhishthira explains to his brothers that he will present himself to King Virata as a Brahmin named Kanka, a courtier skilled in dice. In Kisari Mohan Ganguli's 1896 English translation he says that he will entertain the king and his friends by moving on boards beautiful pieces of ivory, in blue, yellow, red and white colors, by throws of black and red dice. This is the passage popularly cited as \"chess in the Mahabharata.\" It deserves careful reading: The pieces are made of ivory and come in four colors, which would suit a four-player game. They are moved by throws of dice, so luck decides what is played. It occurs in the Virata Parva, a section whose date is not precisely known. Scholars read it in different ways. One view is that it points to a four-handed, dice-driven game like chaturaji. Another is that it describes a race game such as pachisi. A third suggestion is that it is an early form of a chess-like game. What it very probably does not describe is the two-player, no-dice chaturanga of the sixth century. The honest"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":12,"text":"A third suggestion is that it is an early form of a chess-like game. What it very probably does not describe is the two-player, no-dice chaturanga of the sixth century. The honest conclusion is that the Mahabharata knew board games with pieces and dice, and that such games formed part of the background from which chaturanga emerged. A point of dating is useful here. The Mahabharata is believed to have reached something like its final form by the early Gupta period, around the fourth century CE, but parts are much older and parts may be later. A passage cannot therefore be used to date chess to the time of the war itself, which tradition places in a distant age. 4.3 The war as a living chessboard: battle formations While the epic does not show chess being played, it shows armies being arranged like pieces on a board. The Kurukshetra war is full of named formations (vyuhas) that the commanders deploy each day: the Chakravyuha (also Padmavyuha), the circular lotus formation, inside which the young hero Abhimanyu is trapped and killed after he enters knowing how to break in but not how to break out; the Garuda vyuha (eagle), the Kraunca vyuha (heron), the Makara vyuha (crocodile) and others. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":12,"text":"ng how to break in but not how to break out; the Garuda vyuha (eagle), the Kraunca vyuha (heron), the Makara vyuha (crocodile) and others. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 12 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":13,"text":"The point is that the epic's heroes and poets thought of the battlefield geometrically. Commanders positioned elephants, chariots, cavalry and infantry in relation to one another, and those who understood the patterns won. This habit of mind, an army as an arrangement of different units in space, is exactly the idea that a board game turns into rules. A caution is needed. Modern writers love to say \"Kurukshetra was a chessboard\" and to call Krishna a grandmaster who moved the Pandavas like pieces. That is a lovely metaphor, and it is not evidence that chess existed in the epic age. 4.4 Krishna, Radha and the game In later Indian painting, particularly from the Rajput and Pahari schools of the medieval and early modern period, one finds pictures of the divine couple Krishna and Radha seated at a chessboard. The painting called \"Krishna and Radha Playing Chaturanga,\" now widely reproduced, shows the pair on an 8×8 board. These images belong to the tradition of devotional love-play (lila) and show that chess had become, by that period, a graceful courtly amusement associated with romance. They are not ancient evidence, but they are valuable pictures of how the game was imagined in India. A popular school textbook in Rajasthan also"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":13,"text":"amusement associated with romance. They are not ancient evidence, but they are valuable pictures of how the game was imagined in India. A popular school textbook in Rajasthan also states that Krishna and his friends are believed to have played the game in the Mahabharata age; this belongs to tradition, not to the text of the epic. 4.5 Mandodari and the invention of the game (Ramayana tradition) The most popular Indian origin story places the invention of chess in Lanka, in the Ramayana world. It is told like this. Ravana, the ten-headed king of Lanka, is a warrior who knows only conflict. His queen Mandodari is distressed by his constant wars and rage. She wants to turn his attention from real battle to something else. She invents a game of war on a board, with pawns, elephants, soldiers, kings and plenty of strategy, so that her husband can fight his battles on a board and not in the world. According to the story, she soon begins winning against him. The war of the Ramayana happens regardless. A version of the legend adds divine help: Mandodari prays to Ganesha, who teaches Ravana the game and asks him to treat it as a real battlefield. Another version in the same tradition says that the daughter-in-law of Ravana, the wife of"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":13,"text":"to Ganesha, who teaches Ravana the game and asks him to treat it as a real battlefield. Another version in the same tradition says that the daughter-in-law of Ravana, the wife of his son Meghnada, also took up the game. These stories are repeated in popular magazines, newspaper features and a school textbook for Rajasthan's Class 10 physical education course. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 13 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":14,"text":"How should a careful reader treat this? Two points: No ancient text is known to contain it. I did not find a Sanskrit or Persian source for the Mandodari story in the research done for this report. It appears to be a later popular tradition. The Ramayana itself, in its standard versions, is silent about chess. It has become literature in its own right. A 2014 novel, The Queen's Play by Aashish Kaul, retells the story, imagining that the original game was played by four players with dice and that Mandodari invented the two-player version by discarding the dice and promoting the queen figure. That is imaginative fiction, not history. What is interesting about the legend is its meaning. It tells us that Indians understood chess as a substitute for war: a way of draining violence into a board. It also explains the theme that the game was invented by a woman for a restless king, a theme that appears again in Persian and Arabic stories where a sage invents the game to teach a king a lesson. 4.6 Shiva and the Tamil chess legend South Indian temple tradition has its own chess story. In Tamil Nadu, the god Shiva is said to have played chess disguised as a Siddha (a perfected ascetic). He won the game, threw off his disguise and revealed"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":14,"text":"as its own chess story. In Tamil Nadu, the god Shiva is said to have played chess disguised as a Siddha (a perfected ascetic). He won the game, threw off his disguise and revealed himself to everyone; this was followed by a grand wedding with the goddess Rajarajeswari. In memory of the victory, the Lord is worshipped under the name Chaturanga Vallabha Nathar, \"Lord who is the master of chaturanga,\" from the Tamil form chaturangam for the game. The legend is attached to a temple tradition and shows that chess had entered the religious imagination of Tamil culture, where, in later times, the game was a popular pastime. 4.7 The Indian wise man and the Persian king (Sissa) The best-known Arabic and Persian origin story concerns a wise man called Sissa ibn Dahir and a king, whose name varies (Balhait, Shahram, Shihram, Shirham). It ends with the famous wheat-on-the-chessboard reward and is told in Chapter 14, because it is as much a story about mathematics as about chess. An older form of the same legend tells of a queen named Husiya, daughter of Balhait, whose son was killed by a rebel. The news was gently given to the grieving mother by showing her, on the chessboard, how the prince had fallen. The first known writer to record the"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":14,"text":"hose son was killed by a rebel. The news was gently given to the grieving mother by showing her, on the chessboard, how the prince had fallen. The first known writer to record the wheat story is the thirteenth-century biographer Ibn Khallikan (1256). Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 14 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":15,"text":"4.8 Why the legends matter The legends differ in names and places, but they share a pattern: In every case chess is a substitute for violence or a teacher of wisdom. That is how the cultures that played it understood it. Chapter 5. How chaturanga was played 5.1 A warning about the rules No complete Sanskrit rulebook for chaturanga survives from the early period. What we know comes from comparing: 1. the Persian chatrang described in Pahlavi texts; 2. the Arabic shatranj of the ninth and tenth centuries, which is well documented in chess manuals; 3. the rules of the Asian descendants (Burmese, Thai, Chinese and others); 4. the later Indian descriptions of the game (for example in the Manasollasa). Murray, Davidson, Golombek and other historians have each written a reconstruction. They agree on most points, and differ on a few. What follows is the mainstream picture, with the disputes marked. Legend Inventor Purpose of the invention Mandodari A queen Distract a warlike king from real war Ganesha and Ravana A god Teach strategy as a substitute for battle Sissa A wise man Teach a king to value his subjects (and show the value of the lowly pieces) Husiya's story An Indian sage Break news of a death gently through the game Bozorgmehr"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":15,"text":"Sissa A wise man Teach a king to value his subjects (and show the value of the lowly pieces) Husiya's story An Indian sage Break news of a death gently through the game Bozorgmehr (Persia) A minister Win a diplomatic contest of wits Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 15 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":16,"text":"5.2 The board and the setup Board: 8×8 squares (the ashtapada), a single color. Alternating colors came much later. Players: two, one against the other. Pieces: sixteen per side, eight in the back rank and eight foot soldiers in the second rank. They were originally figurative: carved elephants, horses, chariots and soldiers. The Islamic world later made them abstract, probably out of religious reluctance to represent living creatures. Back rank: chariot, horse, elephant, then the royal pair (king and minister), and the same three on the other side, matching the modern setup of rook, knight, bishop and king/queen. One point about the setup in the Arabic game is worth noting: the position of the two kings was not fixed in shatranj, so a player could place the king on either side. Whether this was also true of chaturanga is not known. 5.3 The pieces A few observations: Chaturanga piece Meaning Reconstructed move Modern descendant Raja (king) The ruler One square in any direction King Mantri (minister, also senapati, general) The counsellor One square diagonally Queen (much weaker) Gaja (elephant, also hasti) Elephant corps Disputed; in shatranj, two squares diagonally, leaping Bishop Ashva (horse) Cavalry Two squares in one"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":16,"text":"re diagonally Queen (much weaker) Gaja (elephant, also hasti) Elephant corps Disputed; in shatranj, two squares diagonally, leaping Bishop Ashva (horse) Cavalry Two squares in one direction, then one square at a right angle, leaping Knight Ratha (chariot, also nauka boat in some versions) Chariot corps Any distance along ranks and files Rook Padati (foot soldier, also bhata, sainika) Infantry One square forward; captures one square diagonally forward Pawn Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 16 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":17,"text":"The mantri is a modest piece. The overwhelming power of the modern queen is a European creation of about 1475. The gaja is the greatest mystery. Murray argued that it moved exactly two squares diagonally and could jump over an intervening piece, as the alfil does in shatranj. Others have proposed different moves, such as one square forward or diagonally (like the legs and trunk of an elephant, resembling the silver general of Japanese shogi), or two squares along ranks and files. The varied moves of the elephant in the Asian descendants (the Thai and Burmese elephant resembles the silver-general move, the Chinese elephant is a two-square diagonal piece that cannot jump) show how the move may have changed as the game spread. The ratha has also been debated. The German historian Johannes Kohtz suggested that the original chariot move may have been different from the rook's, but most scholars accept the straight-line move. The padati had no two-square first move. Pawns advanced one square at a time, which made opening play slow. 5.4 The rules of play The following rules are those of shatranj, which historians assume closely resembled chaturanga: No castling. It was invented much later in Europe. No en passant, since there was no"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":17,"text":"e following rules are those of shatranj, which historians assume closely resembled chaturanga: No castling. It was invented much later in Europe. No en passant, since there was no double step. Pawn promotion was to the weak minister (mantri) only. In some descriptions a pawn could promote to the piece originally on its file; the early rule is uncertain. Check was announced, and a king in check had to be saved. A king could not be left in check. Win by checkmate (the king is attacked and cannot escape). Win by baring the king: capturing every opposing piece except the king also won, though this rule may have been added to shatranj and was often countered by a draw if the opponent could bare your king on the next move. Stalemate counted as a win for the player who delivered it in shatranj, a rule that persisted for centuries. The modern rule that stalemate is a draw was fixed only in the early nineteenth century. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 17 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":18,"text":"The Arabic master al-Adli (ninth century) records some further customs in the game, and the later literature gives detailed opening play, endgame studies and chess problems, but those belong to the Islamic stage described in Chapter 9. 5.5 How a game felt Players of reconstructed shatranj or chaturanga report a game very different from modern chess: Slow and positional. With weak pieces (the minister and elephant) and slow pawns, attacks took time to build, and a lead in material was often decisive. Tactics matter less than patience. There is no long-range queen to launch sudden raids. Endgames were central. Because the king had to be mated or the opponent bared, endgame technique was a major part of the art. The literature of the Islamic masters shows that players of the old game developed openings (tabiyat), problem positions (mansubat) and strategy that already resemble what modern players study. 5.6 The four-handed game: Chaturaji The four-player variant, chaturaji (\"four kings\"), was described in detail by al-Biruni in his book on India (about 1030). It is the version that fits the Mahabharata's Kanka passage best: Four players, each with a color (often black, green, red and yellow) and a set of pieces: a king, a horse, a"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":18,"text":"30). It is the version that fits the Mahabharata's Kanka passage best: Four players, each with a color (often black, green, red and yellow) and a set of pieces: a king, a horse, a boat (nauka), an elephant and four pawns. Players sit at the four sides of the board, and two sets of players are allies. Dice decide the move. Two dice were thrown; the number rolled determined which piece could be moved. In one reconstruction with a six-sided die, a 1 or 2 moved the king or a pawn, a 3 the boat, a 4 the horse and a 5 the elephant, while a 6 allowed no move. Some descriptions use four-sided stick dice with a different assignment. Murray believed cube dice were a substitute for older oblong dice. The boat moves two squares diagonally, the same move that Murray assigned to the elephant of shatranj. The term chaturaji itself was also used for a position similar to checkmate (a king reduced to isolation). Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 18 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":19,"text":"As noted in Chapter 2, scholars now regard chaturaji as a dice-driven offshoot of two- player chaturanga, not its ancestor. Still, it kept the memory of the Indian game alive in Indian homes into the twentieth century. 5.7 What changed between chaturanga and modern chess Chapter 6. Why they played: the purposes of the game Chaturanga was never \"just a game.\" In every culture that received it, people gave reasons for playing, and the reasons reveal what the game meant. The sources point to several overlapping purposes. Feature Chaturanga / shatranj Modern chess Board colors One color Alternating light and dark Minister/queen One square diagonally Any distance in any direction Elephant/bishop Short diagonal leap or other short move Any distance diagonally Pawn first move One square only One or two squares En passant None Yes Castling None Yes Promotion To the weak minister To queen, rook, bishop or knight Stalemate A win for the stalemating player (shatranj) A draw Bare king A win Not a win Pace Slow, material-driven Fast and tactical Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 19 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":20,"text":"6.1 A model of war without bloodshed The earliest Sanskrit reference, in Banabhatta's Harshacharita, already presents the game as a peaceful stand-in for war: under a good king, one learns how to arrange a chaturanga only on the board, not by cutting off real limbs. Historians have called this the first known description of chess as a substitute for, or a training in, actual war. It is important not to overstate this. There is no firm evidence that Indian armies used chaturanga as a formal drill. What the evidence shows is that the game was designed as a model of an army and understood by players as a battle in miniature. A king who played could rehearse the thinking that war requires: protecting the ruler, coordinating different kinds of troops, sacrificing material for position, and judging an opponent's plans. 6.2 Education of princes and nobles A second purpose was education. The Persian text Khusraw ud Redag lists chess among the skills that a well-bred young man should master, together with other courtly arts. In India, the twelfth-century encyclopedia Manasollasa (\"Delight of the Mind\" or \"Delight of the Spirit\"), written for the Chalukya king Someshvara III (reigned about 1126 to 1138), treats chess as one of the"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":20,"text":"encyclopedia Manasollasa (\"Delight of the Mind\" or \"Delight of the Spirit\"), written for the Chalukya king Someshvara III (reigned about 1126 to 1138), treats chess as one of the accomplishments and pleasures of a ruler. Scholars have described that work as a \"mirror for princes,\" a handbook for the king's education, and it has a section on chaturanga alongside sections on other games and diversions. 6.3 Diplomacy, prestige and wealth Chess was a tool of diplomacy. The famous Persian story (see Chapter 8) presents the arrival of the game as a gift from an Indian king, accompanied by a challenge. In the Persian tradition, the set sent to Khosrow was made of precious materials: sixteen emerald pieces and sixteen ruby pieces. Whether or not the details are true, the story assumes that chess sets were fit gifts between kings. The archaeological record confirms the point. Museum scholars studying early chess and backgammon pieces from Islamic lands have noted how many were made from ivory and rock crystal, expensive materials, which suggests that chess was an elite pastime. Historians also record that the Abbasid caliph Harun al-Rashid sent diplomatic gifts to Charlemagne's court at Aachen around the year 800, and that a famous"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":20,"text":"was an elite pastime. Historians also record that the Abbasid caliph Harun al-Rashid sent diplomatic gifts to Charlemagne's court at Aachen around the year 800, and that a famous medieval chess set later became attached to Charlemagne's name (see Chapter 7), even though the surviving pieces are much later. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 20 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":21,"text":"6.4 A test of intelligence In the Persian and Arabic stories, chess is repeatedly used as a test of wisdom. An Indian king sends the game to Persia to test the intelligence of the Iranians, and the Persian minister Bozorgmehr passes the test and answers with a game of his own. In another story the game's inventor shows a king how to be wise. In both cases the point is the same: a person who can understand a hidden structure of rules and strategy has real intelligence, and intelligence is power. 6.5 Moral teaching A recurring lesson is that the game teaches humility and care for the weak. In the Sissa legend, the wise man designs a game in which the king, the most important piece, is helpless without the support of every other piece, even the lowliest pawn. The point of the story is to show a proud king that he depends on his subjects. This lesson, that the king is protected by the people he commands, is one of the most persistent themes in the cultural history of chess. 6.6 Courtship, leisure and the pleasures of court life In India chess was also a courtly diversion associated with refined leisure. The Manasollasa describes games among the pleasures of the palace, and a recent scholarly study of another game in the same work,"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":21,"text":"ourtly diversion associated with refined leisure. The Manasollasa describes games among the pleasures of the palace, and a recent scholarly study of another game in the same work, the race game phanjika, was titled \"Gameplay as Foreplay at a Medieval Indian Court,\" a reminder that board games and courtship could go together. The later paintings of Krishna and Radha at a chessboard express the same idea: chess as a graceful setting for lovers. 6.7 Mathematical play: the knight's tour Indian writers did not use the board only for competition. They were fascinated by puzzles, in particular the knight's tour, in which a knight visits every square once. A knight's tour appears in Sanskrit poetics (for example in connection with Rudrata's Kavyalankara, about 900 CE, and its commentaries) and again in the Manasollasa (about 1150). Later Indian authors worked out magic-square tours as well. The chessboard was a field of mathematical beauty as much as a battlefield. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 21 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":22,"text":"6.8 Gambling, risk and religious unease There was always a darker side. Because games had been tied to gambling (the Mahabharata's dice game is the great example), moral and religious authorities were suspicious of them. Buddhist texts list board games among the things monks should avoid. In the Islamic world jurists debated whether chess was lawful, with attention to two questions: whether it involved gambling and whether it distracted a person from prayer. In medieval Europe the Church criticized clergy who played. The attitude of suspicion followed the game wherever it went, and it did not stop people from playing. Chapter 7. The oldest pieces and the oldest pictures 7.1 The surprising geography of the evidence A reader might expect that the oldest chess pieces would be found in India, since that is where the game is believed to have been invented. They are not. The oldest securely identified chess pieces come from Central Asia, from the ruins of Afrasiab near Samarkand in what is now Uzbekistan. India has yielded very few early pieces. One partisan Iranian essay argues that this absence weakens the Indian-origin theory. Most scholars reply that the early Indian pieces may simply have been made of perishable materials (wood,"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":22,"text":"Iranian essay argues that this absence weakens the Indian-origin theory. Most scholars reply that the early Indian pieces may simply have been made of perishable materials (wood, clay) that did not survive, that the written and linguistic evidence points to India, and that the Silk Road explains why pieces appear in Central Asia soon after the game appears in Persia. 7.2 The Afrasiab chessmen (c. 7th–8th century) Where found: the settlement mound of Afrasiab, the ancient city beside modern Samarkand, Uzbekistan. When found: 1977, in excavations by the archaeologist Yuri (Jurij) Buryakov, as part of a large project of the Uzbek Academy of Archaeology. What: seven ivory figures: a king, a counsellor (vizier), a chariot, an elephant, two horsemen and a foot soldier. The set is incomplete (a full set would have thirty-two pieces). Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 22 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":23,"text":"Date: estimates range from the late sixth century to about 760. Most popular accounts say mid-seventh to eighth century. This is the earliest set of chess pieces that specialists broadly accept. Where kept: the State Museum of Samarkand. Why they matter: the pieces are figurative, carved in the shape of real military units, and they correspond closely to the pieces later Persian sources describe. The king, vizier, chariot, elephant, horse and soldier is precisely the cast of chaturanga. The style of some of the pieces reflects Indian artistic conventions, which supports the idea of transmission from India eastward and westward along the Silk Road. An article from Aramco World on the Afrasiab finds quotes a story set in the year 762 about a man who dreamt of a battle whose moves resembled Chinese chess, and who woke to find a set of chessmen buried in a well. It is an example of how quickly chess entered the folklore of the lands it reached. 7.3 The Dalverzin-tepe figures (earlier, but disputed) In 1972, in the ruins of Dalverzin-tepe in Uzbekistan, archaeologists found a small ivory elephant and a bull (or zebu), dated by their finders to as early as the second century CE. Uzbek writers sometimes present them as the oldest chess"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":23,"text":"aeologists found a small ivory elephant and a bull (or zebu), dated by their finders to as early as the second century CE. Uzbek writers sometimes present them as the oldest chess pieces in the world. The Russian chess historian Linder argued that they belong not to chess but to a forerunner of chess. Other scholars say their use as game pieces is simply unknown. The sensible conclusion is that they may be game pieces, they may be part of an earlier, different game, and they cannot yet be counted as chess. 7.4 The Nishapur abstract set (late 8th–9th century) The Metropolitan Museum of Art excavated at Nishapur in eastern Iran in the 1930s and 1940s. In a house datable to the early ninth century, archaeologists found twelve ivory chess pieces that were probably made around the end of the eighth century. These are the earliest abstract pieces known: instead of carved horses and elephants, they have simple geometric shapes. A piece with two small humps stands for the elephant, a block with a slot for the knight, and so on. The change from figures to abstract forms is a sign of the game's passage into the Islamic world. Scholars have suggested that it reflects a reluctance to depict living beings. The abstract style then spread"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":23,"text":"bstract forms is a sign of the game's passage into the Islamic world. Scholars have suggested that it reflects a reluctance to depict living beings. The abstract style then spread widely: later Islamic sets were made of ivory, bone, wood, jet, Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 23 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":24,"text":"glass, ceramic, rock crystal and semiprecious stones. The images displayed above in this conversation include a lapis lazuli elephant piece and Nishapur-type pieces. 7.5 The Mozarab pieces (c. 10th century) In northern Spain, small ivory pieces preserved at a Mozarabic (Christian under Muslim rule) monastery in Leon, often called the pieces of Saint Genadio, may be as old as the beginning of the tenth century. Related pieces are held in the Museu de Lleida in Catalonia and in collections in Kuwait. They show the Islamic abstract style crossing the Pyrenees and entering Christian hands. 7.6 The Venafro pieces (c. 980) At Venafro in southern Italy archaeologists found chess pieces of bone topped with ivory, dated to about 980 CE, now in the Archaeological Museum of Naples. They are among the oldest known European pieces. 7.7 The \"Charlemagne\" chessmen (c. 1100) A massive set of carved elephant-ivory chessmen in the Cabinet des Médailles of the Bibliotheque nationale de France in Paris is famous as the Charlemagne chess set. It is called that because for centuries it was kept at the abbey of Saint-Denis and a legend tied it to the emperor. Charlemagne lived about three centuries before the set was carved and probably never played"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":24,"text":"centuries it was kept at the abbey of Saint-Denis and a legend tied it to the emperor. Charlemagne lived about three centuries before the set was carved and probably never played chess. The pieces are dated to around 1100 and were probably made in southern Italy, perhaps in Salerno. They are rich in detail, with elephants carrying castles on their backs, riders, and a seated king and queen. 7.8 The Lewis chessmen (12th century) The best-loved ancient chess pieces in the English-speaking world are the Lewis chessmen. Found: in 1831, in an underground chamber near Uig Bay on the west coast of the Isle of Lewis in the Outer Hebrides, Scotland. Number: roughly ninety pieces from about four sets, including kings, queens, bishops, knights, rooks and pawns. Most are in the British Museum in London and a smaller group is in the National Museum of Scotland in Edinburgh. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 24 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":25,"text":"Material and origin: carved mainly from walrus ivory in the twelfth century. They are thought to have been made in Norway (the Hebrides were then under Norwegian rule), perhaps in Trondheim, and may have been part of a merchant's stock being carried from Norway to Ireland. Why they are famous: the faces are full of character. The queens sit with a hand pressed to the cheek, the knights ride small horses, and the rooks are fierce warriors biting their shields, the \"berserkers\" of Norse tradition. The bishops are among the oldest known bishops in chess, a sign of how Europe had reshaped the old elephant piece into a figure of its own world. 7.9 Disputed claims for older pieces Popular lists of \"oldest chess pieces\" often include items that should be treated cautiously: A small ivory piece found in 2002 at Butrint in Albania, dated to about 465 CE. If accepted as a chess piece it would push the date of chess back a century, but most specialists are not convinced. A piece with a cross on top found in a Byzantine or Roman palace, which would be older still if it really were a chess piece. A sandstone figurine found in Jordan in 1991 and dated to the eighth century. The elephant and bull from Dalverzin-tepe, discussed above. Until"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":25,"text":"ll if it really were a chess piece. A sandstone figurine found in Jordan in 1991 and dated to the eighth century. The elephant and bull from Dalverzin-tepe, discussed above. Until these are confirmed, the Afrasiab set remains the oldest generally accepted set. 7.10 Where to look for historic pictures This report cannot embed copyrighted museum photographs, but the following are the images worth searching for, with the institution that holds or publishes each: Image Why look at it Where it is Afrasiab ivory chessmen Oldest accepted pieces State Museum of Samarkand; ChessBase article \"The oldest chess pieces in the world\" Nishapur abstract pieces and lapis lazuli elephant First abstract Islamic pieces Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 25 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":26,"text":"Chapter 8. Persia: from chaturanga to chatrang 8.1 The route west The game crossed from India into the Sasanian Empire (224–651) in the sixth century or soon after. Persian sources are unanimous that it came from India, and the Persians kept the Indian name, shortened to chatrang. The usual guess is that merchants, diplomats and travelers carried it, and that the king mentioned in the Persian texts, Khosrow I Anushirvan (reigned 531–579), is the Persian ruler at whose court the game became known. Image Why look at it Where it is Charlemagne chessmen Early European carved set Cabinet des Médailles, Paris Lewis chessmen Most famous medieval pieces British Museum; National Museum of Scotland Krishna and Radha playing chaturanga Indian painting of the game on an 8×8 board Reproduced on World History Encyclopedia Shahnameh miniatures of the Indian envoy at Khosrow's court The arrival of chess in Persia Illustrated Shahnameh manuscripts (mostly from the 14th century onward) Persian miniature of shatranj players How the game was played in 1430 Bayasanghori Shahnameh (1430) Libro de los juegos (Alfonso X, 1283) Medieval Spanish picture book of chess, dice and tables Monastery library of El Escorial Lepakshi relief of Shakuni and"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":26,"text":"hori Shahnameh (1430) Libro de los juegos (Alfonso X, 1283) Medieval Spanish picture book of chess, dice and tables Monastery library of El Escorial Lepakshi relief of Shakuni and Yudhishthira The dice game of the Mahabharata Lepakshi temple, Andhra Pradesh Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 26 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":27,"text":"8.2 The story of Dewasarm and Bozorgmehr The Pahlavi text Wizarishn i Chatrang ud Nihishn i Nev-Ardashir (\"The Explanation of Chess and the Arrangement of Backgammon\") tells the most famous story of the game's arrival. It is a legend, but a rich one. Its outline is this: 1. In the reign of Khosrow, a great king of India, called Dewasarm (also rendered Divsaram or Devisharm), wanted to test the wisdom and knowledge of the Iranians. He set up the game of chatrang with a board and pieces and sent it to Khosrow's court with an ambassador, bringing great gifts. 2. The challenge was this: if the Iranian sages could work out the rules and the meaning of the game, India would pay tribute to Persia. If they could not, Persia would owe tribute to India. 3. Khosrow asked for time. In the version told by later Persian writers he asked for forty days. His wise men tried and failed, until, on the last day, the youngest and wisest of the king's ministers, Bozorgmehr (Wuzurgmihr), stepped forward and explained the game. He is said to have played the Indian envoy and won three times. 4. Bozorgmehr then answered with a challenge of his own: he invented backgammon (nard, in the text called after Ardashir), and sent it to India. The Indian sages"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":27,"text":"won three times. 4. Bozorgmehr then answered with a challenge of his own: he invented backgammon (nard, in the text called after Ardashir), and sent it to India. The Indian sages could not decipher it, and so the honor of the contest went to Persia. The text gives a symbolic account of the new game. According to the Wizarishn, Bozorgmehr built the backgammon board like the earth, with thirty counters like the thirty days and nights of the month, fifteen white like the day and fifteen black like the night, and dice like the revolution of the stars. This is cosmology more than history, but it shows how the Persians read their games as models of the universe. In the tradition retold by the later poet Ferdowsi in the Shahnameh (about 1010), the Indian gift is a chess set whose pieces are made of emeralds and rubies (green against red), and the story includes a framing legend about an Indian king's family quarrel and a mother's grief, in which the game is shown to the queen to explain how her son had died. The details change from version to version, and no Indian source records the Dewasarm story. Scholars generally think the Indian king is probably fictional, or at best an echo of a historical ruler from northern India, such as the"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":27,"text":"Indian source records the Dewasarm story. Scholars generally think the Indian king is probably fictional, or at best an echo of a historical ruler from northern India, such as the Maukhari king of Kannauj, whose embassy may lie behind the legend. Two lessons come from this tale. First, the Persians did not claim to have invented chess; they said it came from India. Second, the game's arrival was remembered as a contest of minds between civilizations. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 27 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":28,"text":"8.3 What the Pahlavi texts tell us about the game From the Wizarishn and related sources, the Persian game appears as follows: The pieces were called shah (king), farzin (counsellor, later vazir), pil (elephant), asp (horse), rukh (chariot) and piyade (foot soldier). The names show that the Persians translated or adapted the Indian roles. The text speaks of a battle between two rulers, with the rooks at the sides and the counsellor beside the king, and describes the game as a likeness of war. It is a two-player game without dice. 8.4 The chess of the heroes Persian literature also treats chess as a mark of a noble education. The Karnamak lists skill at chatrang among the accomplishments of the first Sasanian emperor, Ardashir I, and the Khusraw ud Redag has a boy say that he surpasses his companions at chess. As noted in Chapter 3, these texts probably contain later additions, but they show that by the end of the Sasanian period chess was a standard attribute of the cultured Iranian. 8.5 The Persian claim to chess A minority tradition, found mainly in modern Iranian writing, argues that chess may have had a Persian origin or that the Indian story is overstated. The argument rests on the facts that the earliest pieces are Central"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":28,"text":"odern Iranian writing, argues that chess may have had a Persian origin or that the Indian story is overstated. The argument rests on the facts that the earliest pieces are Central Asian and Persianate (Afrasiab was a Sogdian and Persianate city, under Islamic rule from 712), and that no early pieces have been found in India. The majority view has not changed: the language of the game is Indian, the Persian texts say it came from India, and the earliest literary mentions are Indian. A neutral way to put it is that chess may have crystallized in the borderlands where Indian and Iranian worlds met, and that the precise place of the first board remains uncertain. What is certain is that the Persians gave chess its second great home. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 28 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":29,"text":"Chapter 9. The Islamic world: shatranj and the first chess literature 9.1 From Persia to the caliphate When the Arabs conquered Sasanian Persia in the seventh century, they took over its culture, including chess. Chatrang became shatranj in Arabic, and the pieces mostly kept their Persian names, now Arabized. A Persian folk etymology, charming but false, split the word into shat (\"hundred\") and ranj (\"worries\"), a game of a hundred troubles. The Arabic names of the pieces: The words check and checkmate come from Persian shah and shah mat (\"the king is helpless\" or, in a popular but disputed derivation, \"the king is dead\"). 9.2 The golden age of shatranj (about 800–1000) The ninth and tenth centuries produced the first serious chess literature in history. The tenth-century bibliography called the Fihrist of Ibn al-Nadim (compiled in 988) has a section on chess that lists: al-Adli, Kitab ash-shatranj (\"Book of Chess\"), among the earliest masters and authors; ar-Razi, Latif fi'sh-shatranj (\"Elegance in Chess\"); as-Suli, Kitab ash-shatranj in two volumes; al-Lajlaj, a book of chess positions or problems (mansubat); Aluqlidisi, a collection of chess problems. Arabic / Persian Meaning Later European descendant shah king King (from the"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":29,"text":"l-Lajlaj, a book of chess positions or problems (mansubat); Aluqlidisi, a collection of chess problems. Arabic / Persian Meaning Later European descendant shah king King (from the same word; also \"check\") firz or farzin (also wazir) counsellor Queen fil (with the article, al-fil) elephant Bishop (Spanish alfil, Italian alfiere) faras (or asb) horse Knight rukh chariot Rook (via Italian rocco) baidaq (or piyade) foot soldier Pawn Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 29 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":30,"text":"The great players of the age became celebrities. Abu Bakr as-Suli (died about 946) was so famous at the game that an Arabic proverb said of a very good player, \"He plays chess like as-Suli,\" and the thirteenth-century biographer Ibn Khallikan used his entry as the occasion to tell the story of chess's invention. Players were ranked in grades, with the highest rank held by the very strongest, and the best could play without seeing the board. Blindfold play is attested in early Arabic sources. The Arabic books recorded, for the first time in history: opening analysis (tabiyat), lines of opening play that a student could learn; chess problems (mansubat), composed positions in which White wins or draws; the knight's tour; rules of the game, including stalemate and the bare king. This was the beginning of chess as a field of study. 9.3 The caliphs and the courts Tradition connects several Abbasid caliphs, including Harun al-Rashid, with chess, and the game was a favorite of the courts of Baghdad. Courtiers and scholars gambled on it, wrote about it and used it to display wit. The great collections of Islamic art show elaborate sets in ivory, rock crystal, glass and ceramics, which tell us how prestigious the game was. 9.4 Religious"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":30,"text":"d it to display wit. The great collections of Islamic art show elaborate sets in ivory, rock crystal, glass and ceramics, which tell us how prestigious the game was. 9.4 Religious debate Islamic lawyers disagreed about whether chess was permitted. Some objected because of its association with gambling and the figures of living creatures on early sets, and others because it could make people neglect prayer or waste time. Others allowed it as a harmless intellectual pastime as long as no wager was made. The shift to abstract pieces probably reflects part of this debate. 9.5 Big boards and variants The Islamic world also produced variants of the game. The most famous is the Tamerlane chess (shatranj kamil, \"perfect chess\") of the fourteenth century, played on a larger board of ten by eleven squares with extra pieces, associated with the conqueror Timur. Variants of this kind show that the game was now a living tradition that people felt free to change. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 30 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":31,"text":"9.6 Into Africa, Spain and Sicily With the spread of Islam, shatranj moved westward across North Africa to al-Andalus (Muslim Spain) and to Sicily, and eastward through Central Asia. It was in Spain and Italy that it came into contact with Christian Europe. The Spanish word ajedrez and the Portuguese xadrez are still the Arabic ash-shatranj in disguise, a memory of this transmission. Chapter 10. The eastern branches: Southeast Asia, China, Korea, Japan 10.1 Two roads out of India Murray summarized the spread of the game this way: from India it went west through Persia into the Islamic world and Europe, and east by a second route, probably the same one that carried Buddhism, into Southeast Asia and China. Along the eastern roads the game kept its Indian character in some places and changed radically in others. The prevailing view among historians is that chaturanga is the common ancestor of chess, Chinese xiangqi, Korean janggi, Japanese shogi, Burmese sittuyin, Thai makruk and Cambodian ouk chatrang. The Silk Road and the maritime routes explain how a game could pass between so many cultures. 10.2 Southeast Asia: the closest cousins The Southeast Asian games are the most faithful descendants of the Indian original. Sittuyin"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":31,"text":"w a game could pass between so many cultures. 10.2 Southeast Asia: the closest cousins The Southeast Asian games are the most faithful descendants of the Indian original. Sittuyin (Myanmar). The name means \"the army of four limbs,\" since in Burmese sit means army, and the word echoes the four-part army of chaturanga. In sittuyin the players arrange most of their pieces on their own half of the board before play begins, and the bishop-like piece moves like the \"silver general\" of shogi. Makruk (Thailand). The game of the Thai kingdom retains the slow, old-fashioned shape of shatranj. The minister (met) moves one square diagonally, and the elephant- like piece (khon) moves one square diagonally or one square forward, the same as the silver-general move that many historians believe was one early Indian elephant move. Pawns promote on the sixth rank. The game is still played in Thai parks and shops. Ouk chatrang (Cambodia). The name plainly comes from chaturanga, and the game is another close cousin of makruk. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 31 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":32,"text":"Catur (Malay and Indonesian). The very word catur is the Sanskrit chatur. Co ngu oi (Vietnam). Vietnam also has a colorful tradition of \"human chess\" in which people play the pieces. The elephant piece illustrates how differently the same ancestor developed. Chess historians point to two main versions of the elephant's move: a leap of two squares diagonally (which became the Persian and Arabic fil, and then the European bishop through various changes) and a short step of one square diagonally or forward (the silver general), which went to Burma and Siam and later influenced shogi. The version using the leap of two squares, the alfil, eventually disappeared from play. 10.3 Tibet and Mongolia The Tibetan name for the game, chandaraki, is plainly derived from chaturanga, and the Mongolian shatar is probably from the same root. Murray took the names sittuyin, the Annamese chhoen trang and chandaraki as certain reproductions of the Sanskrit, and the Malay chator and Mongolian shatara as probable ones. 10.4 China: xiangqi Chinese chess, xiangqi (\"elephant game\"), looks different from chaturanga. Its pieces are flat discs marked with characters, and they stand on the intersections of the lines of a board, not in the squares. A river"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":32,"text":"game\"), looks different from chaturanga. Its pieces are flat discs marked with characters, and they stand on the intersections of the lines of a board, not in the squares. A river divides the two halves, and a palace marks the area in which the general and his advisors must stay. Its pieces are the general, two advisors, two elephants (which may not cross the river), two horses, two chariots, two cannons and five soldiers. The relationship to chaturanga is debated: The mainstream view is that xiangqi descends from chaturanga, carried to China along the Silk Road. The chariot, horse, elephant and soldier correspond to the four limbs of the Indian army, and the move of the Chinese elephant (two squares diagonally, but unable to jump) resembles the Indian elephant's move. The minority view, put forward by the Chinese-American writer David H. Li in The Genealogy of Chess (1998), argues that the origin of chess lay in China and that the game spread westwards. Li's idea has not been widely accepted, and it remains a minority position. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 32 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":33,"text":"China had older board games, such as liubo, which existed for centuries before chess and which also used pieces on a board. Chess historians study whether these influenced the Chinese game. A Tang dynasty story, reported in the Aramco World article cited earlier, tells of a man in the year 762 who dreamt of a battle whose moves matched Chinese chess and who woke to find a set of chessmen buried in a nearby well. It shows the game was known in China by the eighth or ninth century at the latest. 10.5 Korea and Japan Janggi in Korea is closely related to xiangqi. Its elephant move was changed into a slightly longer-reaching version of the horse's move. Shogi in Japan is the most dramatically transformed relative. Its pieces are wedge- shaped, with the names on the face, and it has a rule that no other chess-family game shares: a captured piece may be brought back into play on the capturer's side (the drop rule), introduced in later centuries. The silver general and gold general of shogi are recognizable relatives of the Indian elephant and the minister. Chess historians sometimes place all of these games on a genealogical \"tree,\" with chaturanga at the root and branches running to Persia and the West, to Southeast Asia, and to East"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":33,"text":"ess historians sometimes place all of these games on a genealogical \"tree,\" with chaturanga at the root and branches running to Persia and the West, to Southeast Asia, and to East Asia. The details of the branching are still being studied. Chapter 11. Europe: from shatranj to the Queen 11.1 Arrival Chess reached Europe through several doors at once: Muslim Spain (al-Andalus), Sicily and southern Italy, and Byzantium, where the Greek name zatrikion shows the Persian chatrang. It also entered Russia from the east. By about the year 1000 it was known in the courts and monasteries of western Europe. The oldest European pieces (Venafro, the Mozarab pieces) date from the tenth century. As the game moved, it was adapted to Christian Europe's own picture of society: The Persian shah (king) stayed a king, and his name gave Europe the word for the game itself (Old French eschecs, English chess) and for check. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 33 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":34,"text":"The firz or farzin (counsellor) became the queen in many languages, partly because the Arabic word sounded like a Romance word and partly because Europeans liked to imagine the royal pair. The fil (elephant) became the bishop in English, the alfiere (standard-bearer) in Italian, the alfil in Spanish and the fou (jester) in French. Europeans who had never seen an elephant reinterpreted the odd, two-horned shape of the piece. In Russia the old name for elephant survived as slon. The rukh (chariot) became the rook (a tower in several languages). The asp (horse) became the knight. The piyade (foot soldier) became the pawn (from a word for \"foot soldier\"). 11.2 The Church and the game Chess found a place in monasteries and courts, and also drew criticism. The eleventh- century reformer Peter Damian is famous for rebuking a bishop for playing chess. Church authorities worried about gambling and time-wasting. Despite this, chess became a popular activity of nobles and clergy. 11.3 Chess as a mirror of society Medieval Europe loved moralized chess. About 1300 the Dominican friar Jacobus de Cessolis wrote a book that used the pieces as a picture of the social orders: the king, the queen, the bishops, the knights, and the pawns as the"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":34,"text":"00 the Dominican friar Jacobus de Cessolis wrote a book that used the pieces as a picture of the social orders: the king, the queen, the bishops, the knights, and the pawns as the various trades and workers. It was one of the most copied and translated books of the age, and in 1474 the English printer William Caxton produced an English version, The Game and Playe of the Chesse, one of the very first books printed in English. The great illustrated chess manuscript of the age is the Libro de los juegos (\"Book of Games\"), commissioned by King Alfonso X of Castile in 1283, which includes chess problems and charming pictures of Christians and Muslims playing together. 11.4 The revolution of about 1475: the Queen and the Bishop For roughly seven centuries Europe played the old Arabic game, with a weak counsellor (called the queen) and a short-leaping elephant (the bishop). Then, in the later fifteenth century, the rules changed so quickly that the new game was called scacchi alla rabiosa, \"mad queen chess.\" The new rules gave the queen the combined powers of rook and Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 34 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":35,"text":"bishop and gave the bishop a long diagonal range. Pawns gained the optional two-square first move, with the en passant rule following, and castling took its modern shape in the following period. The oldest surviving text that shows the new game is the Catalan poem Scachs d'amor (\"Chess of Love\"), composed in Valencia around 1475 by three poets, Bernat Fenollar, Narcís Vinyoles and Francí de Castellví. It is an allegorical poem about a chess game between Mars and Venus' champions, in which the queen (dama) and bishop already move as they do today. The exact date is a matter of scholarly discussion, but the poem is widely accepted as the earliest text to show the modern rules. It was followed by the printed books that spread them: Francesc Vicent, Llibre dels jochs partits dels schachs (Valencia, 1495), now lost, the first printed treatise on the new chess; Luis Ramírez de Lucena, Repetición de amores y arte de ajedrez (Salamanca, about 1497); Pedro Damiano, a Portuguese author, whose book appeared in Rome in 1512. A popular tale credits Queen Isabella of Castile with making the queen powerful, as a tribute to her own rule. Historians doubt the story. The queen's new powers arose in the literature and playing circles of the Spanish"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":35,"text":"Castile with making the queen powerful, as a tribute to her own rule. Historians doubt the story. The queen's new powers arose in the literature and playing circles of the Spanish and Italian worlds, and Valencia is now celebrated as the cradle of modern chess. The new queen made chess faster and more tactical. It is the greatest single change in the game since it left India, and it is the reason the game has seemed endlessly rich ever since. 11.5 The first modern masters After the new rules, European players began to study the game scientifically: Ruy López de Segura, a Spanish priest, published a book of analysis in 1561, with an opening that still bears his name. The Italian school of the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries (Polerio, Greco and others) developed brilliant attacking play. François-André Philidor, a French composer and chess master, published in 1749 a famous book in which he argued that the pawns are the soul of chess, laying the foundations of positional play. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 35 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":36,"text":"Chapter 12. The modern era 12.1 Standardization The rules were fixed gradually. Stalemate was settled as a draw in the early nineteenth century, replacing the old rule that made it a win or a loss. Clubs, journals and books multiplied, and city teams played each other by correspondence. In 1849 the London makers Jaques brought out the Staunton pattern of pieces, named for the leading English player Howard Staunton, and it was offered to the public on 29 September 1849. Its clear, balanced design became the standard that all international events use today. Staunton also helped organize the first international tournament, held in London in 1851 with sixteen leading players from across Europe, which was won by Adolf Anderssen. 12.2 Champions and federations 1886: Wilhelm Steinitz is recognized as the first official world champion. 1924: the world chess federation, FIDE (Fédération Internationale des Échecs), is founded in Paris. The twentieth century saw the Soviet school of chess, the 1972 world championship match of Bobby Fischer against Boris Spassky, and the long reign of Garry Kasparov. 12.3 Machines In 1997 IBM's Deep Blue defeated Kasparov in a match, a landmark in artificial intelligence. In 2017 DeepMind's AlphaZero taught"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":36,"text":"nd the long reign of Garry Kasparov. 12.3 Machines In 1997 IBM's Deep Blue defeated Kasparov in a match, a landmark in artificial intelligence. In 2017 DeepMind's AlphaZero taught itself chess from the rules alone and defeated the strongest conventional engines, with a style that human players found strikingly original. Today the strongest programs run on phones, and online chess brings millions of players together. 12.4 The return to India The country that gave the world chaturanga took a long time to become a chess power, but it has. 1951: the All India Chess Federation is formed. 1956: India makes its debut at the Chess Olympiad in Moscow. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 36 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":37,"text":"1988: Viswanathan Anand becomes India's first grandmaster, and later wins the world championship five times. 2022: India hosts its first Chess Olympiad, at Chennai, and its teams win bronze medals in the Open and Women's sections. 2024: India wins the gold medals in both the Open and Women's sections at the Budapest Olympiad, and D. Gukesh becomes the world champion at the age of eighteen, the youngest in history. As of December 2024, India had 85 grandmasters. It is a satisfying circle: the game of the \"four limbs\" has come home as one of the strongest chess nations on earth. Chapter 13. The names of chess and its pieces 13.1 The name of the game in many languages Language Name Note Sanskrit chaturanga \"Four-limbed\"; army and game Tamil chaturangam Used in the Shiva legend Hindi / Urdu shatranj Via Persian and Arabic Middle Persian chatrang Pahlavi texts Persian / Arabic shatranj Arabized form Spanish ajedrez From ash-shatranj Portuguese xadrez Same source Greek (medieval) zatrikion From Persian Italian scacchi From shah French échecs From shah English chess From Old French eschecs German Schach From shah Russian shakhmaty From shah Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 37 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":38,"text":"The pattern is striking. Two big families of names spread out from the same root: one from the Sanskrit chaturanga (Persian chatrang, Arabic shatranj, Spanish ajedrez, Malay catur) and one from the Persian shah, \"king\" (Italian scacchi, French échecs, German Schach, English chess, Russian shakhmaty). 13.2 The pieces through the ages Language Name Note Turkish satranç From shatranj Burmese sittuyin \"Army\" Thai makruk Different word, same family Cambodian ouk chatrang From chaturanga Malay / Indonesian catur From chatur Tibetan chandaraki From chaturanga Mongolian shatar Probably from chaturanga Chinese xiangqi \"Elephant game\" Korean janggi Related to xiangqi Japanese shogi \"General's game\" Role Sanskrit (chaturanga) Persian Arabic Medieval Europe Modern English Ruler raja shah shah king King Counsellor mantri farzin firz, wazir fers, dame Queen Elephant gaja pil fil alfin, alfil, fou Bishop Horse ashva asp faras chevalier Knight Chariot ratha rukh rukh roc, tour, rocco Rook Foot soldier padati, bhata piyade baidaq pedon, peon Pawn Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 38 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":39,"text":"Chapter 14. The legend of the wheat on the chessboard 14.1 The story The most famous chess story of all is a mathematical parable. It is told in many versions, and this is the usual one. A wise man named Sissa ibn Dahir invented a new game for an Indian king (named Balhait, Shahram, Shirham or Ladava in different tellings). Perhaps his aim was to teach the king, who was proud and careless, that a ruler cannot survive without his people. The king was delighted by the game and offered Sissa any reward he wished. Sissa's request seemed modest. He asked for one grain of wheat on the first square of the board, two on the second, four on the third, and so on, doubling on every square until the sixty-fourth. The king agreed, almost insulted at the smallness of the request. But when the royal treasurers began to count, they found that the whole kingdom's harvest could not pay the debt. The king saw that the inventor's cleverness in asking was even greater than his cleverness in inventing the game. In some tellings the king rewards him anyway, and in a grim humorous one the king solves his problem by beheading the mathematician. 14.2 The mathematics The number of grains needed is the sum of 1 + 2 + 4 + ... + 2^63, which equals 2^64 − 1,"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":39,"text":"orous one the king solves his problem by beheading the mathematician. 14.2 The mathematics The number of grains needed is the sum of 1 + 2 + 4 + ... + 2^63, which equals 2^64 − 1, or 18,446,744,073,709,551,615 grains, far beyond any harvest in history. The thirteenth- century biographer Ibn Khallikan, who gives the first known written account of the story (in 1256), admits that he consulted accountants in Alexandria in order to grasp the size of the number. They told him to imagine the heap reached at the sixteenth square, 32,768 grains, as a certain measure, and to keep multiplying. The story has been used for centuries to teach exponential growth. Modern writers, including Carl Sagan and the authors of The Limits to Growth, have used the doubling chessboard to explain the dangers of unlimited growth. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 39 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":40,"text":"14.3 History or legend? It is legend. Sissa is regarded as a mythical Brahmin. Until the nineteenth century the story was one of several theories about the origin of chess, and it is now seen as a myth that grew up in the Islamic world. But there is a hint of something real. An earlier Arabic tradition, recorded by the historian al-Masudi, says that shatranj was invented under an Indian king who preferred it to backgammon. The Arabic word for a square of the board, bayt (\"house\"), may support the Indian origin of the calculation. As one modern writer puts it, the story may be fictional but not entirely made up: an early mathematical puzzle connected with the chessboard may have inspired the later legend. Chapter 15. Open questions and competing theories After a century of study, the history of chess still has unsolved puzzles. A fair account should end with them. 1. Where exactly was chess first played? The majority view says northern India. Some argue for the borderlands between India and Iran, and a small minority for China. The earliest physical pieces come from Central Asia, the earliest clear Sanskrit mention from India, and the fullest early stories from Persia. 2. When? The cluster of evidence around 600 CE points to the"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":40,"text":"ces come from Central Asia, the earliest clear Sanskrit mention from India, and the fullest early stories from Persia. 2. When? The cluster of evidence around 600 CE points to the sixth century, but a precursor game might be older. The Dalverzin-tepe figures and the Butrint piece are tantalizing, but unproven. 3. Was there a dice phase? The Cox–Forbes theory said yes. Murray's critique said no. Most scholars now say that two-player, dice-free chaturanga is the original and that chaturaji is a later variant, but the Mahabharata's mention of ivory pieces moved by dice leaves room for older dice-based board games that influenced it. 4. What was the elephant's original move? Two squares diagonally with a leap, a short step diagonally and forward, or something else? The Asian descendants preserve different answers. 5. What were the exact rules? No Indian rulebook of the early period survives, so the rules are reconstructed from Persian and Arabic sources and later descendants. 6. Why so few early Indian pieces? Perhaps because they were made of perishable materials, perhaps because archaeology of this question has barely begun. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 40 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":41,"text":"7. How are the Chinese games related? Descent from chaturanga, independent development, or a shared parent game are all still argued. Glossary Akshauhini: the largest division of the epic army, with equal numbers of chariots and elephants, three times as many horses and five times as many foot soldiers. Alfil: the old elephant piece, which leaps two squares diagonally. Ashtapada: the 8×8 uncolored board used for chaturanga and for dice race games. Chatrang: the Middle Persian form of the game. Chaturaji: the four-handed, dice-driven Indian game described by al-Biruni. Chaturanga: \"four limbs\"; the four-part army and the ancient Indian game. Farzin / Ferz: the Persian counsellor, ancestor of the queen. Gaja: Sanskrit for elephant. Mansubat: Arabic chess problems. Mantri: the minister piece. Padati / Bhata: the foot soldier. Ratha: the chariot. Shah mat: Persian phrase behind \"checkmate.\" Shatranj: the Arabic and Persian name for the old game. Vyuha: a battle formation in the Mahabharata. Sources and further reading Book-length scholarship: H. J. R. Murray, A History of Chess (Oxford, 1913), the foundation of the field. Henry A. Davidson, A Short History of Chess (1949). Harry Golombek, Chess: A History (1976). Richard Eales,"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":41,"text":"Murray, A History of Chess (Oxford, 1913), the foundation of the field. Henry A. Davidson, A Short History of Chess (1949). Harry Golombek, Chess: A History (1976). Richard Eales, Chess: The History of a Game (1985). David H. Li, The Genealogy of Chess (1998), for the minority Chinese-origin argument. John Gollon, Chess Variations: Ancient, Regional, and Modern (1968). Online sources consulted for this report: Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 41 of 42"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":42,"text":"Encyclopaedia Britannica, entries on chess and chaturanga. Wikipedia, articles on Chaturanga, Chaturaji, Shatranj, Chatrang, Alfil, Shakuni, Sissa (mythical brahmin), the wheat and chessboard problem, Scachs d'amor, and Chess in India. Encyclopaedia Iranica, entry \"Wizarishn i Chatrang ud Nihishn i Nev-Ardashir.\" ChessBase articles: \"The oldest chess pieces in the world\" (André Schulz); \"On the origins of chess\" (Sergio Ernesto Negri); \"The millennial journey of chess along the Silk Road\"; the series on Scachs d'amor. Chess Variants Pages, \"Chaturanga\" (comparing Murray, Golombek, Davidson and Gollon). World History Encyclopedia, \"The History of Chess\" (Howard Burton). Aramco World, \"The Game of Kings\" (2009). UNESCO Silk Roads, \"Traditional Strategy Games along the Silk Roads: Chess.\" Edward Winter's Chess Notes and chesshistory.com, for the discussion of the Lothal claim. Burlington Magazine review of the Al-Sabah Collection catalogue on chess and backgammon pieces from Islamic lands. KWABC (Valencia) essays by José Garzón on the cradle of modern chess. Kolachana and Wujastyk, \"Gameplay as Foreplay at a Medieval Indian Court\" (2022), on the Manasollasa. Popular and textbook sources for the Mandodari, Ganesha and Shiva legends"},{"game":"chess","title":"01 · Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess","file":"01-chaturanga-to-chess.pdf","page":42,"text":"ss. Kolachana and Wujastyk, \"Gameplay as Foreplay at a Medieval Indian Court\" (2022), on the Manasollasa. Popular and textbook sources for the Mandodari, Ganesha and Shiva legends (Organiser, Outlook India, RBSE Class 10 textbook) and the review of Aashish Kaul's novel The Queen's Play. A note on limits This report was assembled from the sources above and from standard scholarship. Dates for the oldest pieces, the details of early rules and the content of origin legends vary between sources, and where they do the report says so. Some details in the Persian and Arabic stories are reported as they appear in the traditions, not as verified history. For an assignment, cite the primary scholarly works (Murray, Eales, Golombek) and the original articles rather than this summary. Chaturanga and the Evolution of Chess: A Research Report Page 42 of 42"},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":1,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 1 CHAUSAR, CHAUPAR & PACHISI From India’s cross-shaped race games to modern Ludo A historical research dossier Origins  Mahabharata traditions  Mughal court culture  Rules  Artefacts  Paintings  Colonial adaptations  Modern Ludo Prepared 4 November 2026 Research note: this study distinguishes documented evidence from legend and later popular retellings. Dates attached to artworks and surviving objects refer to those objects, not necessarily to the invention of the game."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":2,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 2 Contents and research approach The dossier is organised as a sequence of focused chapters, from terminology and early evidence through Mughal court culture, surviving objects, colonial-era adaptations and digital Ludo. It includes a rules overview, a timeline, a glossary and a source trail for historical images. 01. How to read this history 02. Executive overview 03. Names and spellings 04. The game family, not one frozen rulebook 05. The oldest evidence: what can be claimed 06. Earlier Indian dice games 07. The Mahabharata dice episode 08. Shakuni and the loaded-dice tradition 09. Dice, chance and royal power 10. The 12th-century M■nasoll■sa and precursors 11. The fifteenth-century horizon 12. The Mughal world 13. Akbar and the Pachisi courtyard 14. Did Akbar use living human pieces? 15. Abu’l-Fazl and the rules 16. How the traditional board is laid out 17. Players, teams and counters 18. Cowrie shells and oblong dice 19. A practical rules overview 20. Captures, safety and strategy 21. Why people played 22. Gender and social life 23. The 18th-century object record 24. The British Museum velvet Pachisi board 25. The V&A embroidered board 26. The 1851 painting and its date 27. Rajas and"},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":2,"text":"ender and social life 23. The 18th-century object record 24. The British Museum velvet Pachisi board 25. The V&A embroidered board 26. The 1851 painting and its date 27. Rajas and courtiers playing Chaupar 28. Photographs of the Pachisi Court 29. Colonial encounters and collecting 30. Thomas Hyde, 1694 31. The road to Ludo 32. The 1891 patent and Alfred Collier 33. How Ludo changed the rules"},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":3,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 3 34. Ludo and Parcheesi 35. Global spread and popular culture 36. Modern digital Ludo 37. Regional variations in India 38. Craft traditions: cloth, wood and ivory 39. The game as art and image 40. Comparing Chausar, Pachisi and Ludo 41. A sample traditional turn 42. A sample modern Ludo turn 43. Timeline: key milestones 44. What the evidence cannot tell us 45. Common myths and corrections 46. How to identify a credible historical image 47. Suggested museum and archive trail 48. The cultural afterlife of the game 49. Conclusion 50. Glossary 51. Research bibliography and source notes 52. Primary visual references and collection links 53. Further reading and verification"},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":4,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 4 01. How to read this history This study follows Chausar/Chaupar, Pachisi and Ludo as related members of a family of Indian cross-shaped race games. They are not interchangeable names in every historical source. Regional rules, equipment and naming vary. The central historical question is not simply “when was Ludo invented?” but how a family of games was played, represented, adapted and commercialised across different societies. Evidence is separated into three levels: surviving objects and architecture; contemporary or near-contemporary written descriptions; and later stories or popular retellings. A board found in a museum can prove that a particular form existed by its documented date. It cannot automatically prove that the same game existed in the Vedic age."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":5,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 5 02. Executive overview Chausar (also written chaupar, chaupad or chausar) and Pachisi are traditional Indian race games played on a cruciform track. A common form uses four players, four counters per player, and cowrie shells or elongated dice to determine movement. Players race around the arms of the cross and bring their counters to the centre or home area. Ludo is a simplified, standardised descendant/adaptation popularised in Britain in the late nineteenth century. The most secure evidence for the recognisable cruciform Chaupar/Pachisi family reaches back to around the fifteenth century, while older Indian dice games and race-game traditions certainly existed. The Mahabharata’s dice match is an important cultural parallel, not proof of an identical board game."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":6,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 6 03. Names and spellings The names appear in multiple transliterations: caupa■/chaupar, chaupad, chausar, chopad, pachisi/pachisi, and regional names such as pagade or pagaday. Spellings shift with language, region and colonial transcription. “Pachisi” is commonly associated with the number twenty-five, traditionally connected with a notable throw or score in some versions; exact explanations differ across sources. “Chaupar” and “Pachisi” are often grouped together in museum descriptions because they share a cross-shaped board and related play mechanics. Specialists caution that historical rules differed, and a bare board may not reveal which variant was played."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":7,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 7 04. The game family, not one frozen rulebook Traditional games were living practices. Boards could be embroidered on cloth, drawn on the ground, or permanently marked on a palace courtyard. Counters might be wood, ivory or other materials; throws could use cowries or oblong dice. A modern boxed game with fixed colours and rules should not be projected backwards onto every earlier version. This matters when interpreting archaeological finds: a cross-shaped pattern can be suggestive, but without a securely associated set of counters, dice and textual explanation, its exact game identity may remain uncertain."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":8,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 8 05. The oldest evidence: what can be claimed A major scholarly reassessment by Jacob Schmidt-Madsen argues that the available evidence securely traces the cruciform game of Chaupar/Pachisi only to the fifteenth century. The article challenges the common assertion that the exact game is demonstrably ancient. Earlier games may have contributed elements, but continuity must be demonstrated rather than assumed. This is the most responsible answer to “what is the oldest Chausar?”: there is no universally agreed single oldest surviving complete set. The earliest secure historical horizon for the recognisable game is late medieval/early modern India, with clearer descriptions and objects from the Mughal and later periods."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":9,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 9 06. Earlier Indian dice games Dice play in South Asia predates the securely documented cross-shaped Pachisi board. The Vedic corpus contains references to gambling with dice, and the Mahabharata makes a dice contest central to its narrative. These references establish the cultural importance of gaming and wagering, but do not describe the later cruciform board, its track, or its four-colour counters. Earlier board-game traditions are also suggested by archaeological finds from the Indus civilisation. Yet identifying the exact rules of a game from isolated boards, pieces or marked surfaces is difficult. The safest account distinguishes the long history of gaming from the later documented history of Pachisi."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":10,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 10 07. The Mahabharata dice episode In the Mahabharata, Yudhishthira is drawn into a game of dice with Shakuni, who plays on behalf of the Kauravas. The stakes escalate from wealth and kingdom to the brothers and Draupadi. Yudhishthira loses, setting in motion the humiliation of Draupadi and the Pandavas’ exile. The episode explores dharma, royal duty, addiction, manipulation and the destructive power of a rigged contest. The story is often linked popularly to Chausar. That connection is culturally powerful, but the epic describes a dice wager, not a sufficiently detailed cross-shaped Pachisi board. It should therefore be presented as a related dice-game tradition, not as a documented match of modern Chausar."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":11,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 11 08. Shakuni and the loaded-dice tradition Popular retellings often say Shakuni used magical or loaded dice, sometimes claiming they were made from the bones of a relative. Such details vary among later tellings and should not be presented as a single universally attested passage of the epic. The central narrative point is that Shakuni’s skill and manipulation make the game profoundly unequal. For a history of Chausar, the episode is valuable because it shows how dice play could symbolise political risk and moral collapse. It does not establish the precise rules of Pachisi or Ludo."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":12,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 12 09. Dice, chance and royal power Dice games can dramatise uncertainty: a ruler may possess armies and wealth yet surrender control through a throw. In epic literature, gambling becomes a metaphor for temptation and the failure of judgement. In courtly settings, games could also serve as recreation, sociability and displays of status. Chausar’s combination of chance and tactical movement made it suitable for extended play. Players had to interpret throws, choose which counter to move and decide when to expose a piece to capture. It was not merely a passive lottery."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":13,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 13 10. The 12th-century M■nasoll■sa and precursors The twelfth-century Sanskrit encyclopaedic work M■nasoll■sa includes descriptions of games, including phañjik■, which scholars have considered when tracing earlier ludic elements. The relationship between phañjik■ and later Chaupar/Pachisi is a question of possible antecedents, not a proven straight line of descent. This is a useful example of how game history works: similar mechanics may appear in different games, and a later game can inherit or recombine older ideas without being identical to them."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":14,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 14 11. The fifteenth-century horizon The fifteenth century is important because specialist research identifies it as the earliest period to which the recognisable cruciform Chaupar/Pachisi can currently be securely traced. The evidence base is not a single “birth certificate”; it is a reconstruction from texts, objects and later descriptions. The game may be older than its surviving evidence. However, historians cannot convert probability into certainty. A careful account says that the game is securely visible by this period, while allowing that its origins may reach further back."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":15,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 15 12. The Mughal world Under the Mughals, Chaupar/Pachisi became associated with courtly leisure. The game’s cross-shaped layout, coloured teams and dice made it visually legible in a palace setting. It could be played on portable textile boards or represented at monumental scale in architecture. Mughal court culture preserved detailed descriptions of recreation, ceremonies and imperial life. The writings of Abu’l-Fazl, Akbar’s court intellectual and biographer, are especially important for understanding games and the emperor’s interests."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":16,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 16 13. Akbar and the Pachisi courtyard At Fatehpur Sikri, Akbar’s sixteenth-century imperial complex contains a courtyard conventionally known as the Pachisi Court. Its paving is laid out in a large grid associated with the game. The court is a remarkable architectural witness to the status of board games in the imperial environment. The court is often dated to Akbar’s building programme at Fatehpur Sikri, principally 1571–1585. It is a monumental playing surface, not a surviving portable board. The site is among the most compelling physical landmarks in the history of the game. Visual archive to consult: see the linked museum and archival records in the final reference chapters. Captions and dates should be taken from the holding institution’s catalogue."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":17,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 17 14. Did Akbar use living human pieces? A famous story says that Akbar used women of the court, often described as courtesans or enslaved women, as life-sized playing pieces who moved across the courtyard. The story appears in popular histories and tourism narratives. Sahapedia discusses it as a legend associated with the site, while other historians have questioned the certainty of the claim. The responsible conclusion is that the courtyard is real and its association with Pachisi is well established; the precise practice of using living people as counters is less securely documented. This report treats it as a reported tradition, not an uncontested fact. Visual archive to consult: see the linked museum and archival records in the final reference chapters. Captions and dates should be taken from the holding institution’s catalogue."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":18,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 18 15. Abu’l-Fazl and the rules Abu’l-Fazl’s account is central to descriptions of the game at Akbar’s court. The game is described with a cross-shaped board, sixteen pieces and three dice in one account of Chaupar. Other forms use cowrie shells or two oblong dice. This variation is important: historical play did not have one universal equipment specification. The game commonly involved four players, each with four pieces. Teams or partnerships could be formed by players sitting opposite one another. Counters moved around the arms of the cross and sought to complete a circuit and enter the central home. Visual archive to consult: see the linked museum and archival records in the final reference chapters. Captions and dates should be taken from the holding institution’s catalogue."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":19,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 19 16. How the traditional board is laid out The classic board is cruciform: four arms meet at a central square. Each arm contains a series of marked spaces. The route is arranged so that players enter from their own starting area, travel around the track, and eventually turn inward toward the centre. Some boards include decorative central motifs and special safe squares. Cloth boards are especially important because they could be folded, carried and stored. Embroidery also transformed the board into a textile artwork. The design was therefore both functional and decorative. ● ● ✚ ■ ■ Simplified cross-shaped concept diagram; not a scale-accurate rule board."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":20,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 20 17. Players, teams and counters A common form accommodates four players, each controlling four counters, for sixteen pieces in total. In partnership play, opposite players cooperate. Counters may be differentiated by colour, material or pattern. Museum examples show how elaborate the pieces could become: carved ivory, painted pigment, lacquer and turned wood all appear in surviving sets. The number and appearance of counters vary by region and rule set. A modern Ludo set’s four colours and four tokens per player reflect a simplified standardisation, not every historical variant."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":21,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 21 18. Cowrie shells and oblong dice Traditional Pachisi may use cowrie shells as casting pieces. The number landing with the opening upward determines a move according to the local scoring table. Chaupar is also associated with elongated, four-sided dice known as pasa. These are unlike the familiar cubic die used in most modern Ludo sets. Because scoring tables vary, a throw’s value cannot be stated as universal. Some versions award extra turns for particular throws; some use specific counts to enter a piece or continue movement. Rules should always be attributed to a particular regional form."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":22,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 22 19. A practical rules overview A common four-player version begins with each player’s four counters in a home area. Players take turns casting cowries or dice. A qualifying throw allows a counter to enter the track. Counters move along a fixed route, usually around the cross and toward the central home. Landing on an opponent’s unprotected counter may send it back to its starting area. Safe squares protect counters from capture. A player wins by bringing all their counters home. Exact entry throws, scoring, bonus turns, capture rules and partnership arrangements differ by tradition. This is a family resemblance, not a single standard rulebook. ● ● ✚ ■ ■ Simplified cross-shaped concept diagram; not a scale-accurate rule board."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":23,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 23 20. Captures, safety and strategy The threat of capture gives the race game its tactical dimension. A player may advance a counter toward home, move a vulnerable counter out of danger, or use a throw to capture an opponent. Safe spaces create temporary refuges. In partnership play, one player may sometimes help the other by occupying strategic squares or coordinating movement. The game mixes luck and choice. Throws are random, but players choose which piece to move and how to balance speed against safety. This helps explain its enduring appeal across ages and social settings."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":24,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 24 21. Why people played Games serve many purposes: amusement, sociability, competition, gambling, teaching, ritual and status display. Chausar/Pachisi could be an intimate family pastime, a public leisure activity or a courtly spectacle. Fine boards and counters made the game an object of craft and prestige. It is tempting to assign one universal purpose, such as military training or religious instruction. Evidence does not justify that for all forms of Chausar. Its most securely visible roles include recreation and, in some contexts, wagering and court entertainment."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":25,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 25 22. Gender and social life The game appears in contexts that cross social boundaries. Courtly objects and accounts point to elite play; colonial-era paintings and later ethnographic documentation show ordinary people playing on the ground or on cloth boards. Women’s participation is sometimes visible in courtly stories and images, although the historical record is uneven. A careful social history avoids assuming that a surviving royal object represents everyday play. Expensive ivory pieces tell us about elite craft; simple cloth boards and cowries better suggest portable, accessible forms."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":26,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 26 23. The 18th-century object record The Ashmolean Museum holds Chaupar playing pieces dated to the second half of the eighteenth century, associated with western India, possibly a Rajasthan court. The sixteen-piece set is carved from ivory and painted or lacquered. Some pieces show courtly scenes, including figures on horseback, elephants, camels or chariots. These objects demonstrate that the counters themselves could be miniature works of art. They also show why a game’s material culture matters: the counters reveal regional craft, status and visual imagination beyond the rules."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":27,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 27 24. The British Museum velvet Pachisi board The British Museum’s velvet Pachisi board is dated broadly to 1800–1899 and was collected in Sri Lanka by Hugh Nevill, who worked in the colonial administration there from 1869 to 1886. The board is embroidered on velvet with silk and metallic thread and measures approximately 51 by 49 centimetres. The object is a valuable example of textile-based play. Its catalogue record also illustrates the movement of Indian games and objects through colonial collecting networks. A museum acquisition location is not necessarily the place where an object was originally made. Visual archive to consult: see the linked museum and archival records in the final reference chapters. Captions and dates should be taken from the holding institution’s catalogue."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":28,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 28 25. The V&A embroidered board The Victoria and Albert Museum catalogue includes a Pachisi board with a cross-shaped playing area and rich textile decoration. The object is a reminder that game boards were not merely diagrams: they could display embroidery, colour, regional design and skilled labour. A cloth board could be rolled or folded and used in domestic settings. When studying a museum object, its catalogue date, place of manufacture, acquisition history and materials should be kept separate. “Collected in” does not automatically mean “made in.” Visual archive to consult: see the linked museum and archival records in the final reference chapters. Captions and dates should be taken from the holding institution’s catalogue."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":29,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 29 26. The 1851 painting and its date An archival image titled “1851 Indian men playing a game of pachisi” shows four men seated around a cross-shaped board. The image is attributed to William Carpenter in the relevant collection record. Its title and catalogue context are useful evidence for nineteenth-century visual documentation of the game. The date should be quoted as the date given by the archive or artwork record, not casually converted into a claim that the game itself began in 1851. A painting is evidence of representation and practice at a moment, not proof of origin. Visual archive to consult: see the linked museum and archival records in the final reference chapters. Captions and dates should be taken from the holding institution’s catalogue."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":30,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 30 27. Rajas and courtiers playing Chaupar A painting offered by Christie’s is catalogued as a Raja and his courtiers playing Chaupar, from the Punjab Hills, Guler or Basohli region, circa 1720–1750. It depicts elite men gathered around a cross-shaped board. The image is significant because it records the game as a courtly social activity. Auction catalogues can provide useful object descriptions and provenance, but their claims should be cross-checked with museum or scholarly sources where possible. The painting’s approximate date is not a date for the invention of Chaupar. Visual archive to consult: see the linked museum and archival records in the final reference chapters. Captions and dates should be taken from the holding institution’s catalogue."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":31,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 31 28. Photographs of the Pachisi Court A photograph of the Pachisi Court at Fatehpur Sikri helps distinguish the surviving architectural site from later illustrations of Akbar playing. The court is a physical monument; the familiar scene of the emperor directing living counters is a reconstructed or retold narrative. A late nineteenth-century photograph of the court and the modern photographs available online are different kinds of evidence. The former documents the site at a historical moment; the latter record its present condition and help visitors understand its layout. Visual archive to consult: see the linked museum and archival records in the final reference chapters. Captions and dates should be taken from the holding institution’s catalogue."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":32,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 32 29. Colonial encounters and collecting During the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, European travellers, administrators and scholars described Indian games. Thomas Hyde’s De Ludis Orientalibus (1694) is an early European work on games from the East. Later writers and collectors documented boards, rules and terminology. Colonial collecting also moved game objects into museums and private collections in Britain and elsewhere. This creates a rich archive, but it also means that provenance, collecting circumstances and cataloguing language deserve scrutiny. Visual archive to consult: see the linked museum and archival records in the final reference chapters. Captions and dates should be taken from the holding institution’s catalogue."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":33,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 33 30. Thomas Hyde, 1694 Thomas Hyde’s De Ludis Orientalibus, published in 1694, is frequently cited in the European study of Asian games. It belongs to an early period of comparative scholarship, when European writers began recording rules and names of games from outside Europe. Hyde’s work is not a modern ethnography, and its terminology may reflect the limits of the author’s sources. Still, it is a landmark in the written reception of Indian board games in Europe."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":34,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 34 31. The road to Ludo The British game Ludo is a simplified adaptation of the Indian Pachisi family. The National Archives’ educational material states that Ludo came to Britain in 1891 and was patented by Alfred Collier. The simplification made the game easier to manufacture and explain to a mass market. Ludo standardised the cross-shaped race track into a compact printed board, used cubic dice and reduced the complexity of traditional scoring. Its commercial success helped make it a familiar family game. Visual archive to consult: see the linked museum and archival records in the final reference chapters. Captions and dates should be taken from the holding institution’s catalogue."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":35,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 35 32. The 1891 patent and Alfred Collier The National Archives identifies 1891 as the year Ludo came to Britain and associates its patent with Alfred Collier. This late Victorian moment is a key point in the transition from a diverse family of Indian games to a mass-produced commercial format. The patent history should be distinguished from the much older history of Pachisi. Collier did not invent the Indian game; the British adaptation reorganised and simplified an existing tradition for a new market. Visual archive to consult: see the linked museum and archival records in the final reference chapters. Captions and dates should be taken from the holding institution’s catalogue."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":36,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 36 33. How Ludo changed the rules Compared with traditional Chaupar/Pachisi, modern Ludo typically uses a square printed board, a single six-sided die, four colours and four tokens per player. Players move from a coloured yard onto a shared loop and then up a private home column. A six commonly allows entry from the yard and an extra turn, although house rules vary. The simplified system removes the traditional cowrie scoring tables and the use of oblong dice. It also standardises the route and safe squares. The result is faster to learn and easier to mass-produce. Visual archive to consult: see the linked museum and archival records in the final reference chapters. Captions and dates should be taken from the holding institution’s catalogue."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":37,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 37 34. Ludo and Parcheesi Parcheesi is an American commercial adaptation of Pachisi, while Ludo is the British adaptation. Both preserve the race-game structure but have their own rule histories and commercial identities. The names are related through the broader Indian source tradition, but the games should not be treated as identical in every rule detail. Commercial versions changed the board, dice, tokens, branding and target audience. These adaptations are part of the game’s global history, not merely footnotes. Visual archive to consult: see the linked museum and archival records in the final reference chapters. Captions and dates should be taken from the holding institution’s catalogue."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":38,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 38 35. Global spread and popular culture In the twentieth century, Ludo became a household game in many countries. Its compact rules and low equipment cost suited family play, schools and social gatherings. Local names and house rules proliferated. Digital versions later moved the game from the tabletop to mobile phones and online multiplayer platforms. The global success of Ludo is an example of cultural translation: a game travels, is simplified and repackaged, and then becomes familiar in settings far from its earlier courtly and domestic contexts. Visual archive to consult: see the linked museum and archival records in the final reference chapters. Captions and dates should be taken from the holding institution’s catalogue."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":39,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 39 36. Modern digital Ludo Digital Ludo reproduces the familiar turn-based race, often adding online matchmaking, chat, rankings, animations and timed turns. The basic loop remains recognisable: roll, choose a token, race, capture and reach home. Mobile play has expanded access but can also change the social experience through remote play and monetised features. Digital versions are not neutral copies. Their rules, matchmaking, rewards and interface design influence how people play. The history therefore continues beyond the printed board."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":40,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 40 37. Regional variations in India Indian forms vary in board size, route, scoring, dice, cowrie counts, safe squares, team structure and terminology. Cloth boards and locally made counters are important in many traditions. Some variants are played by two, three or four participants; others use partnerships. A regional rulebook should identify the community and locality it describes. Avoid presenting one version as “the original Indian rules.” The game’s diversity is part of its history."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":41,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 41 38. Craft traditions: cloth, wood and ivory Boards could be stitched, embroidered, painted or marked on the ground. Counters could be turned from wood, carved from ivory, stained with pigments or made from readily available materials. The contrast between a palace set and a household board reveals how the same game could inhabit different economic worlds. Ivory objects must also be interpreted in their historical context and with contemporary ethical awareness. Museum collections preserve craft histories, but modern production should not encourage trade in protected wildlife materials."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":42,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 42 39. The game as art and image Paintings of Chaupar players are not simply illustrations of rules. They show posture, clothing, social hierarchy, architecture and the place of leisure in courtly life. Embroidered boards reveal the meeting of utility and decorative art. Miniature counters can carry scenes of animals, riders and courtly figures. Art-historical interpretation asks who commissioned an image, where it was made, what audience saw it and whether it depicts a real event or an idealised scene. These questions prevent a painting from being misread as a photograph."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":43,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 43 40. Comparing Chausar, Pachisi and Ludo Chausar/Chaupar and Pachisi share a related cross-shaped race-game structure, but the names can refer to distinct rule traditions. Ludo is a later simplified commercial adaptation. The differences are visible in equipment, scoring, board layout and the complexity of movement. A useful comparison is therefore genealogical rather than absolute: Ludo belongs to the family shaped by Pachisi, while Chaupar and Pachisi are closely related Indian forms with overlapping histories. A single straight-line story would erase regional variation."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":44,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 44 41. A sample traditional turn Imagine a four-player game using cowries. A player casts the shells and consults the local scoring table. If the result permits entry or movement, the player chooses one counter and advances it along the prescribed route. If the counter lands on an opponent outside a safe square, it may capture that counter. The captured piece returns to its starting position. This is a simplified teaching example. Actual rules differ, especially in how throws are scored, how counters enter, and whether a player receives bonus turns. ● ● ✚ ■ ■ Simplified cross-shaped concept diagram; not a scale-accurate rule board."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":45,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 45 42. A sample modern Ludo turn In standard modern Ludo, a player rolls one cubic die. A six may allow a token to leave the yard. The player then moves a token the number of spaces shown, following the shared track. Landing on an opponent’s token on an ordinary square usually sends it home; safe squares protect tokens. Once a token completes the loop, it enters its colour’s home column. The first player to bring all four tokens into the final home space wins. Editions and house rules vary, particularly around entry, bonus turns and blockades. ● ● ✚ ■ ■ Simplified cross-shaped concept diagram; not a scale-accurate rule board."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":46,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 46 43. Timeline: key milestones Before the fifteenth century: dice games and related race-game elements are present in South Asian traditions, but the exact later cross-shaped game is not securely demonstrated. Twelfth century: M■nasoll■sa describes games including phañjik■, considered a possible antecedent. Fifteenth century: earliest secure horizon for the cruciform Chaupar/Pachisi according to modern specialist research. Sixteenth century: Mughal court descriptions and Akbar’s Fatehpur Sikri Pachisi Court. 1694: Thomas Hyde publishes De Ludis Orientalibus. Eighteenth century: surviving elite counters and court paintings. Nineteenth century: textile boards, colonial descriptions and paintings. 1891: Ludo introduced/patented in Britain by Alfred Collier. Twentieth century onward: global mass-market and digital Ludo."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":47,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 47 44. What the evidence cannot tell us The exact inventor of Chausar is unknown. No reliable evidence identifies one person, one court or one year as the moment of invention. A cross-shaped board found without associated equipment may not be securely identified. The Mahabharata dice episode cannot be equated automatically with Pachisi. The living-piece story at Fatehpur Sikri is not equally secure as the courtyard itself. These limits do not make the history less interesting. They show how responsible historical research distinguishes what is known, what is plausible and what remains legend."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":48,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 48 45. Common myths and corrections Myth: Ludo was invented in ancient India under that name. Correction: Ludo is a late nineteenth-century British adaptation of Pachisi. Myth: the Mahabharata explicitly describes the modern cross-shaped Chausar board. Correction: it describes a dice contest, not enough board detail to identify Pachisi. Myth: Akbar’s living counters are certain fact. Correction: the courtyard is real; the human-piece story is debated. Myth: all Chaupar and Pachisi boards follow one rulebook. Correction: regional variants are substantial."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":49,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 49 46. How to identify a credible historical image Check the institution or collection holding the image, its catalogue title, date, material, dimensions, provenance and rights statement. Separate the date of the depicted event from the date of the artwork. A 1915 print showing Akbar in 1575 is an image published in 1915, not a sixteenth-century eyewitness painting. Prefer museum collections, national archives, university repositories and scholarly publications. Commercial image sites can help locate works, but should not be the sole authority for historical claims. Visual archive to consult: see the linked museum and archival records in the final reference chapters. Captions and dates should be taken from the holding institution’s catalogue."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":50,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 50 47. Suggested museum and archive trail Useful collections include the Victoria and Albert Museum, the British Museum, the Ashmolean Museum, the Indian Heritage Centre in Singapore, the Ancient Indian Boardgames digital archive and the National Archives (UK). These institutions provide object records, dates, materials, acquisition details and educational interpretation. When using their images in a public project, follow each institution’s image licensing and reproduction terms. A link to a collection record is not the same as permission to republish a high-resolution image. Visual archive to consult: see the linked museum and archival records in the final reference chapters. Captions and dates should be taken from the holding institution’s catalogue."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":51,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 51 48. The cultural afterlife of the game Chausar and Pachisi persist as more than a set of moves. They are associated with epic storytelling, courtly leisure, textile traditions, colonial collecting, family play and modern entertainment. The same basic tension between chance and choice links a cowrie throw on a cloth board to a six rolled on a phone screen. The game’s history is therefore a history of adaptation. Communities preserved familiar mechanics while changing materials, rules, names and meanings. Visual archive to consult: see the linked museum and archival records in the final reference chapters. Captions and dates should be taken from the holding institution’s catalogue."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":52,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 52 49. Conclusion The most defensible history places the recognisable cruciform Chaupar/Pachisi within the late-medieval and early-modern Indian world, with especially rich evidence under the Mughals. Earlier dice traditions and literary narratives, including the Mahabharata, form an important cultural background but do not prove that the exact later board game existed in epic times. Akbar’s Pachisi Court, museum-held counters and boards, and eighteenth- and nineteenth-century paintings provide tangible evidence. In 1891, Alfred Collier’s British Ludo adaptation simplified the game for mass play. Modern Ludo is thus not a disconnected invention: it is one global descendant of a diverse Indian game family."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":53,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 53 50. Glossary Chaupar/Chaupad: Indian cross-shaped race-game tradition. Chausar: common variant spelling. Pachisi: closely related Indian race game, often associated with a score of twenty-five. Pasa: elongated Indian dice. Cowries: shells used as casting pieces. Counter/token: playing piece moved along the track. Cruciform: shaped like a cross. Safe square: a protected space where a counter cannot be captured. Home/centre: the destination area. Phanjik■: an earlier game mentioned in M■nasoll■sa and discussed as a possible antecedent."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":54,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 54 51. Research bibliography and source notes Jacob Schmidt-Madsen, “The Crux of the Cruciform: Retracing the Early History of Chaupar and Pachisi,” Board Game Studies Journal 15 (2021), pp. 29–77. DOI: 10.2478/bgs-2021-0003. MAP Academy, “Pachisi.” Sahapedia, “Towards a Cultural History of Indian Board Games: Backgammon, Chaupar and Chaturanga.” Ashmolean Museum, “Pieces for the game of chaupar,” dated second half of the eighteenth century. British Museum/Google Arts & Culture, “Velvet Pachisi game board,” 1800–1899. V&A Collections, “Pachisi board.” National Archives (UK), “What can we learn from old board games?”"},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":55,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 55 52. Primary visual references and collection links Victoria and Albert Museum, Pachisi board: https://collections.vam.ac.uk/item/O452950/pachisi-board-pachisi-board-unknown/ . Ashmolean Museum, Chaupar pieces: https://jameelcentre.ashmolean.org/collection/921/object/13240 . British Museum velvet Pachisi board record: https://artsandculture.google.com/asset/velvet-pachisi-game-board/vQHGssW2nMPv3Q . Christie’s, Raja and courtiers playing Chaupar, circa 1720–1750: https://www.christies.com/en/lot/lot-6417048 . Wikimedia Commons, 1851 Indian men playing Pachisi: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1851_Indian_men_playing_a_game_of_pachisi.jpg . Fatehpur Sikri court photograph: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_pachisi_court.JPG ."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":56,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 56 53. Further reading and verification MAP Academy’s Pachisi entry is especially useful for correcting claims about the Mahabharata and the earliest surviving evidence. The 2021 Schmidt-Madsen article provides the specialist argument for a fifteenth-century secure horizon. Sahapedia offers a broader cultural history and discussion of Akbar’s court. The National Archives provides the 1891 Ludo/Alfred Collier account. Museum object records should be consulted directly before reproducing object images or quoting dimensions and dates."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":57,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 57 Illustrated source register Image / object Date / significance Collection or source Embroidered Pachisi board V&A catalogue object; textile board https://collections.vam.ac.uk/item/O452950/pachisi-board-pac hisi-board-unknown/ Men playing Pachisi Archival image titled 1851 https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1851_Indian_men_pla ying_a_game_of_pachisi.jpg Chaupar ivory counters Ashmolean; second half of 18th c. https://jameelcentre.ashmolean.org/collection/921/object/1324 0 Velvet Pachisi board British Museum; 19th c. https://artsandculture.google.com/asset/velvet-pachisi-game-b oard/vQHGssW2nMPv3Q Raja and courtiers playing Chaupar Punjab Hills; c.1720–1750 https://www.christies.com/en/lot/lot-6417048 Pachisi Court, Fatehpur Sikri Monumental court associated with Akbar https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_pachisi_court.JP G Akbar playing with living pieces Later historical illustration, published 1915 https://www.bridgemanimages.com/en/dudley-robert/akbar-pla ying-pachisi-or-chaupur-with-live-pieces-at-fatehpur-sikri-in-15 75-from-hutchinson-s/nomedium/asset/3588308 Image-use note: this dossier supplies collection links and image identifications rather than embedding"},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":57,"text":"e-pieces-at-fatehpur-sikri-in-15 75-from-hutchinson-s/nomedium/asset/3588308 Image-use note: this dossier supplies collection links and image identifications rather than embedding unverified copies of copyrighted museum photography. Check each institution’s current reproduction licence before reuse."},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":58,"text":"CHAUSAR  CHAUPAR  PACHISI  LUDO Page 58 Selected bibliography  Schmidt-Madsen, Jacob. “The Crux of the Cruciform: Retracing the Early History of Chaupar and Pachisi.” Board Game Studies Journal 15 (2021), 29–77. https://doi.org/10.2478/bgs-2021-0003  MAP Academy. “Pachisi.” https://imp-art.org/articles/pachisi/  Sahapedia. Mohit Srivastava, “Towards a Cultural History of Indian Board Games: Backgammon, Chaupar and Chaturanga.” https://www.sahapedia.org/towards-cultural-history-indian-board-games-backgammon-chaupar-and-chaturanga  Ashmolean Museum. “Pieces for the game of chaupar.” Collection record, second half of the 18th century. https://jameelcentre.ashmolean.org/collection/921/object/13240  National Archives (UK). “What can we learn from old board games?” https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/teaching-resources/what-can-we-learn-from-old-board-games/  Indian Heritage Centre, Singapore. “Collection of Gaming Pieces and Dice,” 18th–19th century. https://www.roots.gov.sg/Collection-Landing/listing/1321154  Ancient Indian Boardgames: Digital Documentation. “Chaupar, Pataleshwar.” https://www.indiaboardgamesarchive.in/omekas/s/ancientindianboardgames/item/76  Victoria and Albert Museum. “Pachisi board.”"},{"game":"pachisi","title":"02 · Chausar, Chaupar and Pachisi to Ludo","file":"02-chausar-pachisi-to-ludo.pdf","page":58,"text":"games: Digital Documentation. “Chaupar, Pataleshwar.” https://www.indiaboardgamesarchive.in/omekas/s/ancientindianboardgames/item/76  Victoria and Albert Museum. “Pachisi board.” https://collections.vam.ac.uk/item/O452950/pachisi-board-pachisi-board-unknown/  British Museum / Google Arts & Culture. “Velvet Pachisi game board.” https://artsandculture.google.com/asset/velvet-pachisi-game-board/vQHGssW2nMPv3Q  MAP Academy and museum object records should be read alongside specialist scholarship; popular retellings of the Akbar story should not replace primary evidence. Methodological conclusion The evidence supports a long South Asian history of dice play and a later, securely documented history of the cruciform Chaupar/Pachisi family. The Mughal period provides unusually rich architectural and textual evidence. The nineteenth-century object and image record makes the game’s material culture visible, while the 1891 British Ludo adaptation explains the transition to the familiar modern board. The history is best understood as continuity through adaptation—not as a single invention story."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":1,"text":"GILLI-DANDA  BAT-AND-BALL CULTURES  CRICKET Page 1 GILLI-DANDA TO CRICKET A history of traditional striking games, colonial encounter and the making of modern sport Deep historical research dossier Origins  Regional names  Rules and technique  Mahabharata traditions  Premchand  Early English cricket  Colonial India  Historical paintings and photographs  Modern cricket Prepared 4 November 2026 Evidence policy: this study distinguishes documented history from popular origin claims. It does not present Gilli-Danda as the proven ancestor of cricket. Historical images are identified by collection records; links and rights notes are provided for verification."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":2,"text":"GILLI-DANDA  BAT-AND-BALL CULTURES  CRICKET Page 2 Contents and how to read this study The chapters move from Gilli-Danda’s equipment and regional forms to cricket’s documentary history, colonial introduction to India, visual records and modern formats. The comparison asks whether there is evidence of direct descent, not merely whether the games look alike. 1. The central question: did Gilli-Danda become Cricket? 2. What is Gilli-Danda? 3. Names across India and neighbouring regions 4. The Mauryan-origin claim: what is actually known? 5. The Mahabharata and the Gilli-Danda connection 6. Premchand’s story ‘Gilli-Danda’ 7. How to play: basic equipment and setup 8. Gilli-Danda rules and common variations 9. Skill, technique and strategy in Gilli-Danda 10. Why Gilli-Danda was accessible 11. Similar stick-and-projectile games around the world 12. Tipcat and the European comparison 13. Cricket before it became a formal sport 14. The 1598 Guildford reference 15. Cricket as a children’s game and adult recreation 16. Cricket in the seventeenth century 17. The first Laws of Cricket, 1744 18. Hambledon, MCC and Lord’s 19. Women’s cricket in early England 20. Cricket spreads through the British Empire 21. Cricket arrives in India 22."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":2,"text":"The first Laws of Cricket, 1744 18. Hambledon, MCC and Lord’s 19. Women’s cricket in early England 20. Cricket spreads through the British Empire 21. Cricket arrives in India 22. Indian participation and the sepoys 23. The Parsis and early Indian cricket 24. The Bombay Quadrangular and community cricket 25. Cricket, nationalism and public identity 26. Cricket equipment: from curved bat to modern bat 27. The cricket ball and the development of bowling 28. The evolution of cricket formats 29. Cricket’s global governance and international competition 30. Cricket imagery: Benjamin West’s The Cricketers, 1763 31. Cricket imagery: Rowlandson’s 1811 satire 32. Cricket in India: the 1859 Calcutta Cricket Club photograph 33. Cricket in India: Peshawar, circa 1896 34. Cricket’s earliest images in Australia 35. Cricket and the rise of mass spectatorship 36. Gilli-Danda and cricket: a technical comparison 37. Could Gilli-Danda have influenced Cricket? 38. Why cricket became codified and Gilli-Danda remained local 39. The modern revival of traditional games 40. Safety and responsible play 41. Timeline: Gilli-Danda and related games 42. Timeline: Cricket"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":3,"text":"GILLI-DANDA  BAT-AND-BALL CULTURES  CRICKET Page 3 43. Cricket’s colonial contradictions 44. The transformation from imperial pastime to Indian mass sport 45. What historical pictures can and cannot prove 46. The oldest pictures of Gilli-Danda: limits of the archive 47. A research guide to the most useful cricket images 48. A comparative study: Gilli-Danda, Tipcat and Cricket 49. Glossary of key terms 50. Conclusion: two games, connected by resemblance and history A note on dates The first securely recorded reference to cricket is not the same as the date it was invented. Likewise, the absence of a dated early Gilli-Danda source does not prove the game is recent. Dates for paintings and photographs refer to the image or object, not necessarily the event depicted. 1. The central question: did Gilli-Danda become Cricket? Gilli-Danda and cricket invite comparison. Both involve a player using a long implement to strike a smaller object, and both create a contest between striking and fielding. In Gilli-Danda, the projectile is a short tapered wooden peg called the gilli; in cricket, it is a ball. The visual resemblance is striking, especially when Gilli-Danda is described as “cricket without a ball.” Similarity, however, is not"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":3,"text":"g called the gilli; in cricket, it is a ball. The visual resemblance is striking, especially when Gilli-Danda is described as “cricket without a ball.” Similarity, however, is not proof of descent. To show that one game evolved into another, historians need evidence of transmission: dated descriptions, shared technical vocabulary, documented contact, or a traceable sequence of rule changes. At present, there is no strong primary-source chain demonstrating that English cricket was directly derived from the Indian game Gilli-Danda. The strongest conclusion is more nuanced. Gilli-Danda is an established traditional South Asian striking game with many regional forms. Cricket developed in England and has a traceable documentary history by the late sixteenth century. Both belong to a broad human family of bat-and-projectile games, and colonial contact later brought cricket into India, where it became deeply localised. Claims of direct ancestry should be presented as hypotheses or popular tradition, not established fact. 2. What is Gilli-Danda? Gilli-Danda is a traditional outdoor game played with two pieces of wood. The danda is the longer stick, used as a bat; the gilli is shorter and tapered at both ends. A player uses the danda to"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":3,"text":"a traditional outdoor game played with two pieces of wood. The danda is the longer stick, used as a bat; the gilli is shorter and tapered at both ends. A player uses the danda to flick one end of the gilli so that it rises into the air, then strikes it while airborne to send it away. Opponents may try to catch it, stop it, or return it according to local rules. The equipment is simple and can often be made locally. A short branch can be shaped into a gilli and a longer straight stick can serve as the danda. This low equipment barrier helps explain why the game became associated with open spaces, neighbourhoods and rural play. It can be played without a formal ground, manufactured ball, protective kit or permanent pitch. There is no single worldwide rulebook for Gilli-Danda. Local groups differ in the number of attempts, how distance is measured, how catches are scored, whether the gilli is struck once or multiple times, and how a player is dismissed. A historical account should therefore identify the regional version rather than pretend that one modern description represents every community. 3. Names across India and neighbouring regions The game is known by a wide range of names. Commonly recorded forms include Viti-Dandu in"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":3,"text":"rn description represents every community. 3. Names across India and neighbouring regions The game is known by a wide range of names. Commonly recorded forms include Viti-Dandu in Marathi, Danguli in Bengali, Chinni-Dandu in Kannada, Kitti-Pullu in Tamil, Kuttiyum-Kolum in Malayalam, Gooti-Billa in Telugu, Guli-Badi in Odia and Dandi-Biyo in Nepali. Spellings vary because names are transliterated from different scripts and dialects. Other names are reported across the wider region, including Gulli-Danda in Punjabi and Hindi-speaking areas, Lappa-Duggi in Pashto-speaking contexts, Alak-Doulak in Persian-language descriptions, and Tipcat in English. Similar games are also found in other parts of the world under local names. Similarity across regions does not automatically prove a single point of origin."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":4,"text":"GILLI-DANDA  BAT-AND-BALL CULTURES  CRICKET Page 4 Regional names are not merely vocabulary. They may accompany different stick shapes, scoring systems, number of players and local conventions. The Marathi term Viti-Dandu, for example, is part of a living regional vocabulary in Maharashtra. A good research project should record local pronunciation and spelling from speakers, not rely only on lists copied between websites. 4. The Mauryan-origin claim: what is actually known? Many modern websites and sports organisations repeat the claim that Gilli-Danda is 2,000 or 2,500 years old and dates to the Mauryan Empire. Some describe it as having spread widely under Mauryan rule. These statements are attractive because they give the game a clear ancient pedigree, but the claim is not supported by a securely identified Mauryan inscription, dated illustration, excavated rulebook or contemporary account naming the game. The absence of a surviving ancient record does not prove that the game did not exist. Simple stick games are easy to make and may be passed orally for generations. Their material remains are unlikely to survive. But historians must distinguish a plausible antiquity from a demonstrable date. “It may be very old” is"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":4,"text":"sed orally for generations. Their material remains are unlikely to survive. But historians must distinguish a plausible antiquity from a demonstrable date. “It may be very old” is different from “it is proven to have begun in 500 BCE.” The most defensible wording is that Gilli-Danda is a long-standing traditional game of the Indian subcontinent, with no firmly established invention date. The Mauryan origin is a popular historical claim that requires better primary evidence. The distinction is important because a careful history can celebrate a tradition without inventing certainty. 5. The Mahabharata and the Gilli-Danda connection Some modern descriptions connect Gilli-Danda to a Mahabharata verse mentioning the Pandavas and Kauravas playing with a small wooden object or stick. Indian traditional-games resources sometimes interpret the verse as a reference to a game resembling Gilli-Danda. This is an interesting literary association, but it needs close textual examination before it can be treated as proof. Epic texts have long transmission histories, multiple recensions and layers of commentary. A word translated as a toy, peg, ball or playing object may not describe the exact modern game. Even if the passage describes a"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":4,"text":"ries, multiple recensions and layers of commentary. A word translated as a toy, peg, ball or playing object may not describe the exact modern game. Even if the passage describes a stick-based pastime, it does not necessarily specify the two-stick flick-and-strike mechanics, the scoring system or the regional names used today. The responsible treatment is to include the Mahabharata association as a traditional interpretation, then explain what the passage does and does not establish. It may show that stick-based play or a related pastime was imaginable in epic storytelling. It does not, without further philological evidence, date the complete modern rules of Gilli-Danda to the epic period. 6. Premchand’s story ‘Gilli-Danda’ One of the most valuable literary references to the game is Premchand’s short story “Gilli-Danda.” The story uses a childhood game as a lens through which to examine memory, class, friendship and social hierarchy. It contrasts the apparent equality of childhood play with the inequalities that shape adult life. Premchand’s story is not an archaeological report or a rulebook. Its value is cultural and social: it records the emotional importance of the game in the world of early twentieth-century North Indian"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":4,"text":"not an archaeological report or a rulebook. Its value is cultural and social: it records the emotional importance of the game in the world of early twentieth-century North Indian childhood. The game becomes a shared memory through which characters revisit a time when status seemed less decisive. The story is especially useful for a research chapter on why people played. Gilli-Danda needed little equipment and could be played in ordinary open spaces. It could create a common activity across neighbourhood groups, even while the adult world around the players remained unequal. Literature preserves experiences that official sports histories often overlook. 7. How to play: basic equipment and setup The basic equipment consists of a danda, a longer straight stick, and a gilli, a short piece of wood tapered at both ends. Dimensions vary. Many descriptions give a danda of roughly two feet and a gilli of a few inches, but these are practical examples rather than a universal standard. Players need an open space where the struck gilli will not hit bystanders, windows or traffic. A small notch or shallow depression may be used as a resting point for the gilli. The striker places one end of the gilli in the notch and taps or flicks the"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":4,"text":", windows or traffic. A small notch or shallow depression may be used as a resting point for the gilli. The striker places one end of the gilli in the notch and taps or flicks the raised end with the danda. As the gilli rises, the striker swings again to hit it farther away. In some versions the first flick and second strike are separate actions; other local rules allow more complex sequences."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":5,"text":"GILLI-DANDA  BAT-AND-BALL CULTURES  CRICKET Page 5 Players divide into a striking side and fielding side, or take turns individually. The fielders try to catch the airborne gilli or return it to the striking point. The striker’s score may depend on distance, successful contacts or the number of times the gilli is hit in the air. Since the rules vary, the players should agree on the local scoring method before starting. Research caution: identify the local rule version or image catalogue record before generalising. A modern reconstruction is useful for demonstration but is not automatically an ancient or universal form. 8. Gilli-Danda rules and common variations In one widely described format, the striker gets a limited number of attempts to flick and hit the gilli. If the gilli is caught by a fielder before it touches the ground, the striker is out. If it is not caught, the distance from the striking point to where the gilli lands can be measured using the length of the danda or gilli, and that count becomes the score. Other versions allow the striker to hit the gilli repeatedly in the air, with additional points for consecutive contacts or for distance. Some use a return throw: a fielder retrieves the gilli and attempts to hit"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":5,"text":"to hit the gilli repeatedly in the air, with additional points for consecutive contacts or for distance. Some use a return throw: a fielder retrieves the gilli and attempts to hit or throw it back toward the base. The striker may be dismissed if the return lands within a defined area or if a fielder completes a required action. These variations are not errors. They are a normal feature of informal games that are transmitted through play rather than standardised competition. A serious study should describe the version actually observed, including the local name, number of players, dimensions of the sticks, scoring, dismissal and winning condition. 9. Skill, technique and strategy in Gilli-Danda Gilli-Danda requires timing and hand-eye coordination. The striker must judge the angle at which the gilli will rise, the speed of the first tap and the moment to swing the danda. A clean strike sends the small wooden object away; a mistimed swing may miss completely or send it only a short distance. Fielders must react quickly to a small, fast-moving object. Catching requires anticipation, positioning and coordination. In versions where a return throw matters, fielders must also judge distance and aim. The game can therefore develop"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":5,"text":". Catching requires anticipation, positioning and coordination. In versions where a return throw matters, fielders must also judge distance and aim. The game can therefore develop agility and spatial judgement even though it uses minimal equipment. Strategy depends on the local scoring system. A striker may prioritise a high, difficult-to-catch hit or a low, controlled shot into open space. Fielders may spread out to cover distance or position themselves near likely landing zones. Because rules differ, the strategic priorities differ too. 10. Why Gilli-Danda was accessible The game’s material simplicity is one of its most important social features. Two pieces of wood can be fashioned without specialist manufacturing. The playing area can be a lane, courtyard, field edge or open patch of ground. There is no need for a regulation pitch, expensive ball, gloves, pads, helmet or a large team. This accessibility helps explain why Gilli-Danda became associated with childhood and informal neighbourhood play. Children could make equipment from available branches and organise a game with friends. Local rules could be negotiated on the spot. The game was adaptable to the space and number of players available. Accessibility should not be"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":5,"text":"hes and organise a game with friends. Local rules could be negotiated on the spot. The game was adaptable to the space and number of players available. Accessibility should not be romanticised as evidence that every child had equal access or that the game was played identically everywhere. Local customs, gender expectations, caste and class boundaries, school schedules and the availability of safe open space all shaped who could play. The game’s low cost made it possible, but society still shaped participation. 11. Similar stick-and-projectile games around the world Gilli-Danda is not the only game in which a small stick or object is struck with a larger stick. Related or comparable games have been recorded in Europe and Asia. Tipcat in England, Lippa in Italy, Bilharda in parts of Spain and Portugal, and other local games share some mechanics with the South Asian game, although their equipment and rules can differ. Such global parallels raise a historical question: did these games spread from one origin, or did similar games emerge independently because sticks and small projectiles are common materials? Both possibilities may apply in different cases. Without a documented route of transmission, similarity alone cannot settle the"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":5,"text":"sticks and small projectiles are common materials? Both possibilities may apply in different cases. Without a documented route of transmission, similarity alone cannot settle the question."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":6,"text":"GILLI-DANDA  BAT-AND-BALL CULTURES  CRICKET Page 6 Comparative history is still valuable. It shows that bat-and-projectile play is a broad human practice, not the property of one nation. A comparison can examine the shape of the projectile, whether it is tossed or flicked, how the striking implement is used, how distance is scored and what role fielders play. 12. Tipcat and the European comparison Tipcat is a traditional European game in which a small piece of wood is struck so that it jumps into the air and is then hit with a longer stick. The mechanics make it an especially close comparison with Gilli-Danda. English-language descriptions sometimes use “tipcat” as a translation or equivalent for Gilli-Danda. However, “tipcat” has its own European history and local variants. A shared English label does not establish that the Indian game and the European game were historically identical or that one directly produced the other. The comparison should be treated as a study of related mechanics and possible cultural contacts, not a proven family tree. The presence of similar games across Europe and Asia makes a direct Gilli-Danda-to-Cricket claim more complicated. Cricket’s early documentary record is English. The question is"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":6,"text":". The presence of similar games across Europe and Asia makes a direct Gilli-Danda-to-Cricket claim more complicated. Cricket’s early documentary record is English. The question is whether cricket developed from local English bat-and-ball traditions, whether any Indian game influenced it, or whether broad similarities reflect independent development. The current documentary record supports caution. 13. Cricket before it became a formal sport Cricket’s exact invention is unknown. The International Cricket Council notes the commonly held view that the game may have emerged among children in the Weald, a wooded region of south-east England, during Saxon or Norman times. This is a hypothesis about the game’s early environment, not a dated invention record. Cricket likely developed from earlier bat-and-ball or club-and-ball activities. Some accounts suggest a relationship to bowls, in which a batter intervenes to prevent a ball reaching a target. The precise path is uncertain. Early games were likely less standardised than modern cricket, with local equipment and rules. The earliest documentary evidence is much firmer than the origin story. A court record from Guildford, dated 1598, records John Derrick recalling that he and school"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":6,"text":"ent and rules. The earliest documentary evidence is much firmer than the origin story. A court record from Guildford, dated 1598, records John Derrick recalling that he and school friends had played “creckett” there about fifty years earlier. This is widely treated as the earliest definite written reference to cricket. Research caution: identify the local rule version or image catalogue record before generalising. A modern reconstruction is useful for demonstration but is not automatically an ancient or universal form. 14. The 1598 Guildford reference The Guildford reference comes from a legal proceeding over land. John Derrick, a coroner, gave evidence that as a boy he and other schoolboys had played “creckett” on the disputed land. The record is dated 15 January 1598 in the modern calendar, although older dating conventions can produce a 1597 date in some accounts. This evidence is valuable because it is not a later legend about the game’s origin. It is a contemporary legal record in which cricket is mentioned incidentally. The witness’s recollection places the game in the mid-sixteenth century, around 1550, at Guildford in Surrey. The document does not tell us the rules, number of players, shape of the bat or exact form of the"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":6,"text":"laces the game in the mid-sixteenth century, around 1550, at Guildford in Surrey. The document does not tell us the rules, number of players, shape of the bat or exact form of the wicket. It proves that a game called cricket was being played in that place by the time of the witness’s childhood. It is an early documentary anchor, not a complete description of early cricket. 15. Cricket as a children’s game and adult recreation The ICC’s historical overview notes that the first reference to cricket as an adult sport dates to 1611, while a dictionary from the same year defined it as a boys’ game. This suggests a transition from childhood play toward adult participation, although the process was not a single event. By the seventeenth century, cricket was developing into a village sport. Local matches could involve communities and attract wagers or patronage. Travel limitations kept early competition geographically concentrated, but the game gradually spread beyond its original south-eastern English heartland."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":7,"text":"GILLI-DANDA  BAT-AND-BALL CULTURES  CRICKET Page 7 The distinction between children’s play and adult sport is important when comparing cricket with Gilli-Danda. Both could be played informally by young people, but cricket developed institutions, written rules, clubs, patrons and eventually professional players. That institutional history helped cricket become a codified sport. 16. Cricket in the seventeenth century During the seventeenth century, cricket became more visible in English village life. Local experts could be employed as players, and the game began to attract patrons and wagers. The earliest county-named match known to the ICC took place in 1709, showing that cricket had begun to move beyond purely local contests. Early cricket did not look exactly like the modern game. Bowling was initially delivered along the ground, and bats were shaped more like curved hockey sticks. The wicket and rules also changed. The straight bat became more useful as bowlers began pitching the ball rather than rolling it. The seventeenth century was therefore a period of transition from folk play toward organised competition. The game’s growing popularity created pressure for common rules, more reliable equipment and recognised clubs."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":7,"text":"a period of transition from folk play toward organised competition. The game’s growing popularity created pressure for common rules, more reliable equipment and recognised clubs. These changes gradually made cricket portable as a standardised sport. 17. The first Laws of Cricket, 1744 The first widely recognised written Laws of Cricket were drawn up in 1744. The ICC’s historical summary identifies this as a major milestone. Written laws made it easier for teams from different places to agree on the same contest and settle disputes about dismissal, scoring and the dimensions of play. The laws were amended in 1774. The ICC notes changes including the introduction of leg-before-wicket, a third stump and a maximum bat width. These developments show that the game was still being negotiated and redesigned in response to new tactics and equipment. Codification distinguishes cricket’s institutional history from the more flexible rule traditions of Gilli-Danda. Gilli-Danda’s variation is part of its local life; cricket’s governing institutions increasingly sought uniformity so that matches could be compared across places and seasons. 18. Hambledon, MCC and Lord’s The Hambledon Club in Hampshire became a focal point for cricket in the"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":7,"text":"ought uniformity so that matches could be compared across places and seasons. 18. Hambledon, MCC and Lord’s The Hambledon Club in Hampshire became a focal point for cricket in the late eighteenth century. The ICC describes it as the game’s centre for roughly thirty years before the formation of the Marylebone Cricket Club and the opening of Lord’s Old Ground in 1787. The Marylebone Cricket Club became the custodian of the Laws of Cricket. This gave the game an institutional centre and helped stabilise rules. The club’s influence grew as cricket spread through Britain and later across the empire. Clubs and grounds mattered because they created continuity. They provided places to play, membership structures, records, equipment standards and social networks. Cricket’s later global spread depended not only on the bat and ball but also on these institutions. 19. Women’s cricket in early England The ICC records the first known women’s cricket match in 1745 in Surrey. This is a significant milestone because it shows that cricket was not exclusively a male activity even in the eighteenth century. Women’s cricket has a long history, although it often received less institutional recognition and coverage. Historical paintings and reports"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":7,"text":"tivity even in the eighteenth century. Women’s cricket has a long history, although it often received less institutional recognition and coverage. Historical paintings and reports can reveal how women’s participation was represented. Some depictions are satirical or caricatured, while others record actual matches. The image titled “Rural Sports or a Cricket Match Extraordinary” by Thomas Rowlandson (1811), held by the National Gallery of Art, is a notable example of a humorous visual treatment of cricket. Women’s cricket developed through clubs, touring teams and eventually international competition. A complete history must not treat women’s participation as a recent addition. It should instead examine how opportunities, resources, media attention and governing structures changed over time."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":8,"text":"GILLI-DANDA  BAT-AND-BALL CULTURES  CRICKET Page 8 20. Cricket spreads through the British Empire Cricket spread internationally through British colonial and commercial networks. The ICC notes that it reached North America through English colonies in the seventeenth century, the West Indies through colonists, India through East India Company mariners, Australia around the beginning of colonisation in 1788, and New Zealand and South Africa in the early nineteenth century. Imperial expansion created routes for soldiers, sailors, administrators, merchants and settlers to carry the game. Cricket was often presented as a marker of British identity and social discipline. Yet local communities did not simply receive the game passively; they adapted it, formed teams and made it their own. The spread of cricket was therefore both a colonial history and a history of local agency. The game could represent imperial hierarchy, but it could also become a platform through which colonised people challenged social barriers and built new forms of public identity. 21. Cricket arrives in India The Board of Control for Cricket in India identifies 1751 as the first recorded cricket match in India, played between British Army and civilian or settler"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":8,"text":"ricket arrives in India The Board of Control for Cricket in India identifies 1751 as the first recorded cricket match in India, played between British Army and civilian or settler groups. The date is an important early milestone in the documented history of cricket on the subcontinent. Cricket was introduced by British East India Company personnel, soldiers, sailors and settlers. Early matches were largely played within European communities. Indians initially watched these contests, but local participation grew over time, including among soldiers and urban groups. The BCCI history page identifies the Calcutta Cricket Club, established in 1792, as a major milestone. It describes the club as the second-oldest cricket club in the world after MCC. These dates help trace cricket’s institutional development in colonial India. Research caution: identify the local rule version or image catalogue record before generalising. A modern reconstruction is useful for demonstration but is not automatically an ancient or universal form. 22. Indian participation and the sepoys The spread of cricket in India was not confined to British officers. The BCCI notes reports that Indian soldiers, including sepoys, took up the game and sometimes played"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":8,"text":"he sepoys The spread of cricket in India was not confined to British officers. The BCCI notes reports that Indian soldiers, including sepoys, took up the game and sometimes played with enthusiasm. Some accounts describe Indian soldiers as energetic cricketers and record matches between Indian and European military personnel. Such participation complicates a simple story of cricket as a game imposed from above. Indian players learned the sport, developed skill and entered spaces that colonial authorities had initially controlled. Participation could be shaped by military hierarchy, but it also offered opportunities for recognition and competition. Cricket’s adoption by Indian communities gradually transformed its meaning. A sport associated with British identity became a vehicle for Indian social life, public prestige and eventually national representation. This process took place over generations, not in a single moment. 23. The Parsis and early Indian cricket The Parsi community of Bombay played a major role in the early development of Indian cricket. Parsi merchants and reformers encountered the game in the colonial city and organised clubs. The Oriental Cricket Club, founded in the 1840s, is commonly cited as an early Indian"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":8,"text":"Parsi merchants and reformers encountered the game in the colonial city and organised clubs. The Oriental Cricket Club, founded in the 1840s, is commonly cited as an early Indian cricket club, although the precise chronology of early clubs can be complicated by short-lived organisations and changing names. The Parsis’ adoption of cricket was both recreational and social. It allowed a community to engage with a prestigious colonial institution while building its own clubs and public identity. Parsi teams later toured England, demonstrating that Indian players could compete in the heartland of the sport. The story of Parsi cricket is important because it shows local appropriation rather than passive imitation. Indian communities used cricket to build institutions, cultivate public reputation and negotiate their place in colonial society."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":9,"text":"GILLI-DANDA  BAT-AND-BALL CULTURES  CRICKET Page 9 24. The Bombay Quadrangular and community cricket In late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century Bombay, cricket competitions developed around community-based teams. The Bombay Quadrangular grew from contests involving Europeans and Parsis, later expanding to include Hindu and Muslim teams. The competition reflected the city’s social organisation and the role of community identity in public sport. These tournaments could create intense rivalry and large audiences. They also reflected the limits of colonial and communal structures: teams were organised around social categories rather than simply neighbourhoods or sporting ability. The competition is a valuable lens for studying both the popularity and the politics of cricket in urban India. Over time, criticism grew of communal selection and the social divisions embedded in the tournament. The Quadrangular was eventually replaced by other formats. Its history shows how cricket could both unite spectators through shared excitement and reproduce the divisions of the society in which it was played. 25. Cricket, nationalism and public identity By the early twentieth century, cricket had become more than a colonial pastime in India."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":9,"text":"sions of the society in which it was played. 25. Cricket, nationalism and public identity By the early twentieth century, cricket had become more than a colonial pastime in India. Indian teams and players used the game to demonstrate skill, organisation and collective pride. Touring sides and public matches created opportunities for Indian audiences to see local players compete against British teams. Cricket’s symbolism was complex. It was associated with British institutions and rules, yet Indian success could challenge colonial assumptions about racial and cultural superiority. Players could use a sport introduced by the empire to claim recognition within and beyond imperial society. After independence in 1947, cricket increasingly became part of national culture. The Indian team’s achievements, domestic competitions and later World Cup victories helped turn the sport into a shared public spectacle. The story is not a simple conversion from colonial sport to national sport; it is a long process of appropriation and reinvention. 26. Cricket equipment: from curved bat to modern bat Early cricket bats were shaped more like curved sticks, reflecting the fact that bowlers initially rolled the ball along the ground. As bowling"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":9,"text":"ment: from curved bat to modern bat Early cricket bats were shaped more like curved sticks, reflecting the fact that bowlers initially rolled the ball along the ground. As bowling changed to pitching after about 1760, a straighter bat became more useful. The ICC identifies this change as a major technical turning point. Modern cricket bats are made from willow, traditionally English willow or Kashmir willow, with a flat striking face and a handle designed for grip and control. The ball has a cork core and tightly wound layers covered by leather. Stumps and bails form the wicket, while protective equipment has become increasingly important as bowling speeds rose. Equipment changes altered tactics. A straight bat made defensive and driving strokes more effective; improved protective gear allowed batters to face faster bowling; lighter and stronger materials changed the balance of power. Cricket’s evolution is partly a history of materials science and manufacturing. 27. The cricket ball and the development of bowling Early cricket involved underarm or rolled deliveries. As bowlers began pitching the ball, the batter needed a different technique and the bat shape changed. Over time, bowling developed into a sophisticated discipline"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":9,"text":"led deliveries. As bowlers began pitching the ball, the batter needed a different technique and the bat shape changed. Over time, bowling developed into a sophisticated discipline involving line, length, swing, seam movement, spin and changes of pace. The ball’s construction affects its behaviour. The seam can influence movement through the air or off the pitch, while wear and polishing create different conditions for bowlers. Rules governing the ball, pitch and delivery action have been revised as the game evolved. Bowling innovation has repeatedly forced changes in batting technique and regulation. Cricket is therefore a contest not only between two teams but between evolving technologies and tactics. The same dynamic—players adapting to new methods—appears in the informal rule changes of traditional games, though cricket’s institutional record is much more extensive."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":10,"text":"GILLI-DANDA  BAT-AND-BALL CULTURES  CRICKET Page 10 28. The evolution of cricket formats Cricket developed from local matches into longer multi-day contests, then into limited-overs formats and Twenty20. Test cricket became the traditional international form, with matches lasting up to five days. One-day international cricket introduced a fixed number of overs per side, making matches more predictable in length. Twenty20 cricket, introduced at professional level in the early twenty-first century, compressed the game into a short evening format. It encouraged aggressive batting, specialist roles, new fielding strategies and commercial entertainment. Franchise leagues expanded the sport’s audience and player economy. Each format changed how audiences experienced cricket. Test matches reward endurance and tactical adjustment over time; one-day cricket balances accumulation and acceleration; T20 foregrounds rapid scoring and high-impact moments. The game’s core laws remain recognisable while the experience changes dramatically. 29. Cricket’s global governance and international competition The formation of international cricket structures helped standardise competition across countries. The Imperial Cricket Conference was"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":10,"text":"bal governance and international competition The formation of international cricket structures helped standardise competition across countries. The Imperial Cricket Conference was established in 1909 by England, Australia and South Africa; it later became the International Cricket Council. Membership expanded as more nations gained Test status and international recognition. International cricket became a stage for national identity. Tours, bilateral series and world tournaments allowed countries to compete in front of mass audiences. The game’s global governance also had to address scheduling, player eligibility, umpiring standards, anti-corruption, safety and the commercial rights of competitions. The international game remains uneven in resources and visibility. A few countries command large audiences and revenues, while associate nations work to expand infrastructure and recognition. The history of cricket is therefore not simply a story of universal spread; it is also a story of unequal development and ongoing debates about access. 30. Cricket imagery: Benjamin West’s The Cricketers, 1763 Benjamin West’s painting The Cricketers, dated 1763, is one of the important early works depicting cricket as a fashionable activity. The"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":10,"text":"ery: Benjamin West’s The Cricketers, 1763 Benjamin West’s painting The Cricketers, dated 1763, is one of the important early works depicting cricket as a fashionable activity. The National Portrait Gallery record identifies the sitters as Ralph Izard and friends and describes the work as an oil painting on canvas. The painting captures cricket among educated and wealthy young men. The UK government’s 2022 notice about the painting’s export bar described it as showing the evolution of cricket from a rustic pastime toward a sport adopted by the aristocracy in the eighteenth century. It is valuable because it places cricket within the social world of elite leisure. Use the painting as evidence of how cricket was represented in 1763, not as the earliest image of cricket ever made. The painting’s subject and date are well documented, but the game itself had already been played for generations. Its value lies in showing cricket’s changing social status. Research caution: identify the local rule version or image catalogue record before generalising. A modern reconstruction is useful for demonstration but is not automatically an ancient or universal form. 31. Cricket imagery: Rowlandson’s 1811 satire Thomas Rowlandson’s Rural Sports or a"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":10,"text":"construction is useful for demonstration but is not automatically an ancient or universal form. 31. Cricket imagery: Rowlandson’s 1811 satire Thomas Rowlandson’s Rural Sports or a Cricket Match Extraordinary, dated 1811 and held by the National Gallery of Art, depicts a cricket match in an exaggerated comic style. The scene includes spectators and a playful, chaotic representation of sporting activity. Satirical images can reveal contemporary attitudes as much as the mechanics of play. Rowlandson’s work can be used to discuss the public culture surrounding cricket, including spectatorship, gender, fashion and the humour attached to sport. It should not be treated as a literal record of a standard match. The National Gallery of Art identifies the image as public domain under its open-access policy. It is a useful example of a historical cricket image that may be suitable for reproduction, subject to the institution’s current terms."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":11,"text":"GILLI-DANDA  BAT-AND-BALL CULTURES  CRICKET Page 11 32. Cricket in India: the 1859 Calcutta Cricket Club photograph The National Army Museum holds a photograph titled Members of the Calcutta Cricket Club, 1859. The collection record identifies it as a photograph made in India in 1859 and notes the role of cricket in colonial military and social life. The image is important because it is an early photographic record connected to organised cricket in India. It shows the club world that helped establish the sport in colonial cities. The museum’s contextual note also explains that Indians increasingly took up the game during the nineteenth century. The photograph should be captioned with its exact title, date and holding institution. It is not an image of the first match in India, nor does it represent all cricket players. It is a specific visual record of a club community. Research caution: identify the local rule version or image catalogue record before generalising. A modern reconstruction is useful for demonstration but is not automatically an ancient or universal form. 33. Cricket in India: Peshawar, circa 1896 The National Army Museum also holds a photograph titled A cricket match, Peshawar, 1896 (circa). The catalogue"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":11,"text":"ncient or universal form. 33. Cricket in India: Peshawar, circa 1896 The National Army Museum also holds a photograph titled A cricket match, Peshawar, 1896 (circa). The catalogue describes it as a photograph from the North-West Frontier region and places it in an album compiled by an officer of the Indian Army. This image is useful for studying the spread of cricket beyond the major presidency cities. It also shows how military networks helped carry the game into different parts of the subcontinent. The museum’s note describes the increasing participation of Indian soldiers and the wider popularity of cricket by the late nineteenth century. Because the image is catalogued as circa 1896, the date should remain approximate. A careful historical caption preserves that uncertainty instead of converting it into an exact year. 34. Cricket’s earliest images in Australia The State Library of New South Wales describes a series of watercolours of Hyde Park painted in 1842 by John Rae as the earliest known images of cricket in Australia. Thomas Lewis painted a match in 1843, probably involving a local team and a British regiment stationed in Sydney. The same archive notes S. T. Gill’s lithograph of the 1857 intercolonial match between New"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":11,"text":"tch in 1843, probably involving a local team and a British regiment stationed in Sydney. The same archive notes S. T. Gill’s lithograph of the 1857 intercolonial match between New South Wales and Victoria, and an image of the 1862 match involving the first English team to tour Australia. A photograph of the 1862 match is described as one of the earliest photographs of a cricket game taken in Australia. These images show how cricket’s visual history travelled with the game. Watercolours, lithographs and photographs each preserve different aspects of play and spectatorship. They also document cricket’s role in colonial societies beyond India and England. Research caution: identify the local rule version or image catalogue record before generalising. A modern reconstruction is useful for demonstration but is not automatically an ancient or universal form. 35. Cricket and the rise of mass spectatorship As cricket became more organised, it attracted larger crowds and more extensive press coverage. Grounds became civic landmarks, while newspapers reported scores, players and controversies. Spectatorship turned cricket into a public ritual, especially in cities where clubs and transport made regular attendance possible. Mass media"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":11,"text":"d scores, players and controversies. Spectatorship turned cricket into a public ritual, especially in cities where clubs and transport made regular attendance possible. Mass media transformed the relationship between players and audiences. Radio brought live commentary to people who could not attend; television made technique and drama visible; digital platforms created real-time statistics, highlights and global fan communities. In India, cricket’s mass audience became especially powerful after independence and expanded with television, sponsorship and professional leagues. The sport’s popularity is now linked to national identity, celebrity culture, advertising and entertainment industries as well as athletic competition."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":12,"text":"GILLI-DANDA  BAT-AND-BALL CULTURES  CRICKET Page 12 36. Gilli-Danda and cricket: a technical comparison Both games use a striking implement and a moving object, but their mechanics differ. Gilli-Danda begins with a small tapered peg that is flicked into the air and then struck. Cricket begins with a bowler delivering a ball toward a batter who defends or attacks a wicket. In Gilli-Danda, distance and catches may determine scoring; in cricket, runs, wickets, overs and boundaries structure the contest. The fielding roles are also different. Gilli-Danda can be played with flexible numbers and local fielding conventions. Cricket has eleven players per side in its standard formal version, specialised positions and codified dismissals. Cricket’s ball can bounce, swing and seam; the wooden gilli has different flight characteristics and is often struck from a stationary base. The comparison is useful because it reveals a shared family resemblance without erasing distinct histories. A game can resemble another in one mechanic while differing in the structure that makes it a sport. The existence of a striking stick in both does not establish direct descent. 37. Could Gilli-Danda have influenced Cricket? It is possible that informal stick"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":12,"text":"at makes it a sport. The existence of a striking stick in both does not establish direct descent. 37. Could Gilli-Danda have influenced Cricket? It is possible that informal stick games contributed to the wider culture of bat-and-ball play in different societies. But a specific claim that Gilli-Danda directly produced cricket would require evidence that the game or its rules travelled from South Asia to England before the earliest cricket references, or that English players adopted a recognisably Indian form. The known documentary history of cricket points to south-east England by the sixteenth century. The history of Gilli-Danda is much harder to date because it was an informal folk game with limited written documentation. This imbalance makes it difficult to establish a direction of influence. Popular Indian sports writing sometimes calls Gilli-Danda the ancestor of cricket and baseball. Such claims should be attributed to the source making them and clearly labelled as a theory. The more cautious conclusion is that the two games share mechanics and belong to the broader history of striking games, while direct ancestry remains unproven. Research caution: identify the local rule version or image catalogue record before"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":12,"text":"ics and belong to the broader history of striking games, while direct ancestry remains unproven. Research caution: identify the local rule version or image catalogue record before generalising. A modern reconstruction is useful for demonstration but is not automatically an ancient or universal form. 38. Why cricket became codified and Gilli-Danda remained local Cricket developed clubs, patrons, professional players, written laws, dedicated grounds and national governing bodies. These institutions made it possible to organise matches between distant teams, preserve records and compare performances. Commercial interest and public prestige reinforced the process. Gilli-Danda largely remained an informal game transmitted through family, neighbourhood and community play. Its rules could change with local preference, and it did not develop a comparable global institutional structure. This does not mean it lacked skill or cultural value; it means its social organisation followed a different path. The difference also reflects power and resources. Colonial institutions, urban clubs, print media and later broadcasting helped cricket expand. Traditional games often received less institutional investment and were more vulnerable to changes"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":12,"text":"itutions, urban clubs, print media and later broadcasting helped cricket expand. Traditional games often received less institutional investment and were more vulnerable to changes in land use, schooling, urbanisation and the availability of open spaces. 39. The modern revival of traditional games In recent decades, educators, cultural organisations and traditional-sports groups have promoted games such as Gilli-Danda as part of intangible cultural heritage. Demonstrations, festivals and school programmes can preserve knowledge of equipment and rules that might otherwise be lost. Revival raises practical questions. Should organisers standardise rules to create tournaments, or preserve local variations? Should equipment dimensions be fixed for safety and fairness? How can children learn the game in dense urban settings where open space is limited? These decisions shape what version of the tradition survives. A strong preservation programme records local knowledge before standardising it. It can document multiple regional rule sets, oral histories, names, stick-making techniques and memories from older players. Standardised competition can then be presented as one modern format rather than the only authentic version."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":13,"text":"GILLI-DANDA  BAT-AND-BALL CULTURES  CRICKET Page 13 40. Safety and responsible play Gilli-Danda involves a hard wooden object moving quickly through the air. Players should use a clear open area away from roads, windows, animals and spectators. Fielders should stand at a safe distance and agree on a direction of play. A player should never swing the danda when another person is within striking range. Modern cricket also involves high-speed projectiles and requires safety equipment, particularly for batters facing fast bowling. Helmets, pads, gloves, abdominal guards and protective rules developed as the game became faster and more competitive. The contrast reflects different equipment and levels of formalisation. Safety should be included in any contemporary revival of Gilli-Danda. The game’s low cost is an advantage, but low-cost equipment still requires clear boundaries, safe distances and adult supervision when children play. 41. Timeline: Gilli-Danda and related games Ancient period: claims of Mauryan-era Gilli-Danda circulate in modern accounts, but no securely identified contemporary source establishes the exact game and rules. Epic references to stick-based play are sometimes interpreted as related, but require"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":13,"text":"ccounts, but no securely identified contemporary source establishes the exact game and rules. Epic references to stick-based play are sometimes interpreted as related, but require philological caution. Medieval and early modern periods: informal stick-and-projectile games likely existed in multiple regions, but their documentation is fragmentary. Nineteenth century: colonial ethnography, travel writing and local-language literature increasingly record games and childhood recreation, although the evidence is uneven. Early twentieth century: Premchand’s short story “Gilli-Danda” provides a notable literary treatment of the game and its social meanings. Modern period: traditional-games organisations, educational projects and cultural initiatives document regional names and rules. The game continues in local communities and is promoted through revival efforts. No single date can currently be treated as its definitive invention year. 42. Timeline: Cricket c. 1550: the Guildford witness later recalled playing cricket as a boy around this period. 1598: the Guildford court record provides the earliest definite written reference. 1611: the first known adult-sport reference and a dictionary definition as a boys’ game are noted in cricket"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":13,"text":"ildford court record provides the earliest definite written reference. 1611: the first known adult-sport reference and a dictionary definition as a boys’ game are noted in cricket histories. Seventeenth century: village cricket develops and adult participation expands. 1709: the earliest known match using county names is recorded. 1744: first written Laws of Cricket. 1745: first known women’s match in Surrey. c. 1760: pitched bowling replaces rolling along the ground, encouraging the straight bat. 1774: laws are amended, including LBW and wicket changes. 1787: MCC is founded and Lord’s Old Ground opens. 1751: first recorded cricket match in India, according to BCCI. 1792: Calcutta Cricket Club is established. 1891: not a cricket date but a useful comparison point for the standardisation of other traditional games; cricket’s modern formats develop later. 43. Cricket’s colonial contradictions Cricket was used by British administrators and commentators as a symbol of discipline, fair play and imperial culture. The game was sometimes presented as a means of teaching colonised people British values. Such rhetoric was part of the wider colonial project and should not be repeated uncritically. At the same time, Indian players and"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":13,"text":"ans of teaching colonised people British values. Such rhetoric was part of the wider colonial project and should not be repeated uncritically. At the same time, Indian players and communities made cricket their own. They formed clubs, challenged exclusion, built public followings and used sporting success to assert capability and status. The game could become a site of negotiation and resistance as well as a colonial institution. The National Army Museum’s historical notes on early cricket in India quote colonial commentary that explicitly links cricket to the history of British colonisation. That material is valuable precisely because it reveals the ideology surrounding the game. It should be read critically alongside Indian accounts and the achievements of Indian players."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":14,"text":"GILLI-DANDA  BAT-AND-BALL CULTURES  CRICKET Page 14 44. The transformation from imperial pastime to Indian mass sport Cricket’s Indian transformation took place through schools, clubs, princely patronage, community organisations, military teams, journalism and public competitions. Indian players became skilled participants and spectators developed their own loyalties. The game’s meaning changed as it moved beyond the European enclaves where it had first been organised. After independence, the national team became a powerful symbol of the new country. International victories and prominent players helped turn cricket into a shared national spectacle. The 1983 Cricket World Cup victory is often treated as a watershed in the sport’s popular culture, while later television and professional leagues expanded its commercial reach. Cricket’s Indian identity is not a contradiction of its English documentary origins. Games are transformed by the communities that play them. Cricket in India now has its own heroes, institutions, rivalries, styles and social meanings. 45. What historical pictures can and cannot prove A painting can reveal how a game was imagined, who was represented playing and what social status the activity carried. A"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":14,"text":"anings. 45. What historical pictures can and cannot prove A painting can reveal how a game was imagined, who was represented playing and what social status the activity carried. A photograph can record a particular team, ground or match with greater documentary precision, but its date and caption still need verification. An engraving may reproduce an earlier painting and therefore have a different publication date from the scene it depicts. Images should be catalogued with the holding institution, artist or photographer, date, medium, accession number and rights information. If an image depicts an earlier event, state both dates clearly: for example, “a nineteenth-century illustration of an eighteenth-century match.” The oldest known surviving image is not necessarily the oldest image ever made. It is the earliest currently identified in the collections and scholarship consulted. This distinction is essential when writing about “oldest pictures” of either Gilli-Danda or cricket. 46. The oldest pictures of Gilli-Danda: limits of the archive Compared with cricket, Gilli-Danda has a much thinner historical image archive. It was often played informally in streets, fields and courtyards, using temporary or locally made equipment. Such"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":14,"text":"cricket, Gilli-Danda has a much thinner historical image archive. It was often played informally in streets, fields and courtyards, using temporary or locally made equipment. Such play was less likely to be commissioned as a painting or photographed by colonial studios than elite sport or organised cricket matches. Modern educational and traditional-games websites publish photographs and diagrams of Gilli-Danda, but a photograph of a contemporary demonstration is not evidence of the game’s appearance centuries ago. The date and provenance of every purported historical image should be checked before calling it the oldest. Where an early image cannot be verified, the honest approach is to show a modern documented demonstration and label it clearly, then place it beside historical literary references and comparative images of related stick games. It is better to acknowledge an archival gap than to invent an ancient painting. Research caution: identify the local rule version or image catalogue record before generalising. A modern reconstruction is useful for demonstration but is not automatically an ancient or universal form. 47. A research guide to the most useful cricket images Benjamin West’s The Cricketers (1763), held in the"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":14,"text":"for demonstration but is not automatically an ancient or universal form. 47. A research guide to the most useful cricket images Benjamin West’s The Cricketers (1763), held in the Smithsonian’s National Portrait Gallery record, is a key early painting of cricket among elite young men. The UK government’s 2022 export-bar notice explains its significance in showing the sport’s changing social status in the eighteenth century. Thomas Rowlandson’s Rural Sports or a Cricket Match Extraordinary (1811), in the National Gallery of Art, offers a satirical view of cricket and spectatorship. The Government Art Collection’s engraving The Cricket Match between Sussex and Kent, at Brighton was published in 1849 and reproduces an earlier painting exhibited in 1846. For India, the National Army Museum’s Members of the Calcutta Cricket Club, 1859 and A cricket match, Peshawar, 1896 (circa) are valuable photographic records. The State Library of New South Wales provides early Australian images, including John Rae’s 1842 watercolours and a photograph of the 1862 match."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":15,"text":"GILLI-DANDA  BAT-AND-BALL CULTURES  CRICKET Page 15 48. A comparative study: Gilli-Danda, Tipcat and Cricket Gilli-Danda and Tipcat share a distinctive action: a short piece is made to leap into the air and then struck with a longer stick. Cricket differs because the ball is delivered by a bowler and the batter’s main task is to defend a wicket or score runs. The comparison therefore belongs to a broad family of striking games, not necessarily a single line of descent. Other bat-and-ball games such as rounders, stoolball and baseball have their own documented histories and rule structures. They may share equipment concepts without sharing a direct ancestor. The development of sport often involves borrowing, parallel invention and local adaptation. A comparative table should focus on mechanics, equipment, team size, scoring, dismissal, field shape and institutional history. This makes it possible to identify genuine similarities while avoiding a simplistic claim that all bat games are versions of one ancient sport. 49. Glossary of key terms Gilli: the short tapered wooden peg used in Gilli-Danda. Danda: the longer stick used to flick and strike the gilli. Viti-Dandu: a Marathi name for the game. Danguli: a Bengali name."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":15,"text":"li: the short tapered wooden peg used in Gilli-Danda. Danda: the longer stick used to flick and strike the gilli. Viti-Dandu: a Marathi name for the game. Danguli: a Bengali name. Dandi-Biyo: a Nepali name. Tipcat: an English-language name for a related European game. Wicket: the three stumps and two bails defended by the batter in cricket. Bowler: the player who delivers the ball. Batter: the player who attempts to score runs and protect the wicket. Fielder: a player who attempts to stop the ball and dismiss the batter. Over: a set of six legal deliveries in modern standard cricket. Codification: the process of writing and standardising rules. Provenance: the documented history of an object’s ownership and movement. Primary source: evidence created in the period being studied, such as a court record, photograph or contemporary account. Retrospective tradition: a later story about an earlier event that must be evaluated against contemporary evidence. 50. Conclusion: two games, connected by resemblance and history Gilli-Danda is a traditional South Asian game whose strength lies in simplicity, regional variety and social memory. Its equipment can be made from two pieces of wood, and its rules are transmitted through local play. It"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":15,"text":"game whose strength lies in simplicity, regional variety and social memory. Its equipment can be made from two pieces of wood, and its rules are transmitted through local play. It appears in modern cultural writing, literature and traditional-games initiatives, but its exact invention date remains unknown. Claims of a Mauryan origin or direct descent from it to cricket need stronger primary evidence. Cricket has a much clearer documentary chronology. The 1598 Guildford court record gives an early written reference; the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries saw the game develop into organised adult competition; the 1744 Laws and the formation of MCC in 1787 helped codify it. Cricket reached India in the eighteenth century and was transformed by Indian communities over the nineteenth and twentieth centuries. The two games should be studied together, but not collapsed into one origin story. They share the human pleasure of striking a moving object, judging distance and competing with others. Their histories show how local play can remain flexible while another game becomes institutional, codified and global. The relationship is meaningful even when direct ancestry cannot be proved."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":16,"text":"GILLI-DANDA  BAT-AND-BALL CULTURES  CRICKET Page 16 At a glance: Gilli-Danda and Cricket Feature Gilli-Danda Cricket Projectile Short tapered wooden gilli Leather-covered ball with cork core Striking implement Long wooden danda Flat-faced willow bat Start of play Gilli is flicked into the air, then struck Bowler delivers ball toward batter Scoring Often distance, catches or local hit-count rules Runs, boundaries, extras and wickets Fielding Flexible numbers and local roles Eleven players per side in standard formal cricket Rules Regional and locally negotiated Written Laws, governing bodies and match regulations Historical record Longstanding folk tradition; exact origin date uncertain Documentary reference by 1598; formal laws in 1744 Institutional path Primarily community and cultural preservation Clubs, professional leagues, international governance Visual explanation: Gilli-Danda A simplified sequence is: (1) rest the tapered gilli on a notch or small base; (2) tap one end so it rises; (3) strike it while airborne; (4) fielders attempt a catch or return; (5) score using the local distance or hit-count convention. The exact sequence varies by region. Visual explanation: Cricket A standard delivery sequence is: (1) bowler"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":16,"text":"or return; (5) score using the local distance or hit-count convention. The exact sequence varies by region. Visual explanation: Cricket A standard delivery sequence is: (1) bowler runs in and delivers the ball; (2) batter defends the wicket or hits into the field; (3) batters may run between wickets; (4) fielders retrieve and return the ball; (5) innings continue until a dismissal or over limit. Formal cricket uses a defined pitch, wickets, boundaries and eleven players per side."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":17,"text":"GILLI-DANDA  BAT-AND-BALL CULTURES  CRICKET Page 17 Historical paintings, photographs and visual archive This register identifies historically useful images and their institutional records. Some records provide downloadable or open-access images; others may have reproduction restrictions. Always check the holding institution’s rights statement before publishing an image. No undated internet repost should be labelled “the oldest picture.” Benjamin West, The Cricketers (1763) Oil painting showing cricket among elite young men; National Portrait Gallery record. https://npg.si.edu/object/npg_UK990702 Thomas Rowlandson, Rural Sports or a Cricket Match Extraordinary (1811) Satirical cricket scene; National Gallery of Art identifies the image as public domain/open access. https://www.nga.gov/artworks/31931-rural-sports-or-cricket-match-extraordinary Members of the Calcutta Cricket Club (1859) Photograph, India, 1859; National Army Museum collection record; out of copyright. https://collection.nam.ac.uk/detail.php?acc=1984-07-9-63 A cricket match, Peshawar, circa 1896 Photograph, North-West Frontier; National Army Museum collection record; out of copyright. https://collection.nam.ac.uk/detail.php?acc=2001-02-335-29 The Cricket Match"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":17,"text":"circa 1896 Photograph, North-West Frontier; National Army Museum collection record; out of copyright. https://collection.nam.ac.uk/detail.php?acc=2001-02-335-29 The Cricket Match between Sussex and Kent, at Brighton (published 1849) Engraving based on a painting exhibited in 1846; Government Art Collection. https://artcollection.dcms.gov.uk/artwork/7112/ Early cricket images in Australia (1842–1862) State Library of New South Wales collection essay: John Rae watercolours, Thomas Lewis, S. T. Gill and early match photography. https://www2.sl.nsw.gov.au/archive/discover_collections/society_art/cricket/early/viewer/early-images.html Gilli-Danda / Viti-Dandu: contemporary documentation Bharatiya Khel resource with regional names, rules and contemporary photographs; not an ancient image archive. https://bharatiyakhel.iksindia.org/gilli-danda-viti-dandu/ Gilli-Danda: D'Source, IIT Bombay Educational documentation of equipment, team roles and gameplay; useful for a modern rules illustration. https://dsource.in/resource/indian-games/outdoor-games/gilli-danda"},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":18,"text":"GILLI-DANDA  BAT-AND-BALL CULTURES  CRICKET Page 18 Selected source bibliography  International Cricket Council, “History of Cricket: Early Cricket (Pre 1799).” https://www.icc-cricket.com/about/cricket/history-of-cricket/early-cricket  Board of Control for Cricket in India, “History.” https://www.bcci.tv/news/article/history  Early Cricket, “A brief narrative history — Folk Cricket 1550 to 1720,” including the Guildford court reference. https://www.earlycricket.uk/index.php/narrative-history/  D'Source, IDC School of Design, IIT Bombay, “Gilli Danda.” https://dsource.in/resource/indian-games/outdoor-games/gilli-danda  Bharatiya Khel, “Gilli Danda/Viti Dandu.” https://bharatiyakhel.iksindia.org/gilli-danda-viti-dandu/  Vyuha Games, “Gilli-Danda.” Useful modern synthesis; explicitly notes the lack of a single documented origin. https://www.vyuha.games/games-guide/gilli-danda  Traditional Games Federation of India, “Gilli Danda.” Use regional-name and equipment information critically, as its ancient-origin claim is not independently established. https://www.traditionalgamesindia.com/games-list/gilli-danda/  Madras Courier, “The Ancient Rural Sport of Gilli Danda,” 19 February 2018."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":18,"text":"claim is not independently established. https://www.traditionalgamesindia.com/games-list/gilli-danda/  Madras Courier, “The Ancient Rural Sport of Gilli Danda,” 19 February 2018. https://madrascourier.com/insight/the-ancient-rural-sport-of-gilli-danda/  National Portrait Gallery (Smithsonian), Benjamin West, The Cricketers, 1763. https://npg.si.edu/object/npg_UK990702  National Gallery of Art, Thomas Rowlandson, Rural Sports or a Cricket Match Extraordinary, 1811. https://www.nga.gov/artworks/31931-rural-sports-or-cricket-match-extraordinary  National Army Museum, Members of the Calcutta Cricket Club, 1859. https://collection.nam.ac.uk/detail.php?acc=1984-07-9-63  National Army Museum, A cricket match, Peshawar, circa 1896. https://collection.nam.ac.uk/detail.php?acc=2001-02-335-29  Government Art Collection, The Cricket Match between Sussex and Kent, at Brighton, published 1849. https://artcollection.dcms.gov.uk/artwork/7112/  State Library of New South Wales, “Early cricket images.” https://www2.sl.nsw.gov.au/archive/discover_collections/society_art/cricket/early/viewer/early-images.html  Premchand, “Gilli-Danda,” short story. Consult a reliable published translation or the original Hindi text for literary analysis."},{"game":"cricket","title":"03 · Gilli-Danda and Cricket","file":"03-gilli-danda-to-cricket.pdf","page":18,"text":"iety_art/cricket/early/viewer/early-images.html  Premchand, “Gilli-Danda,” short story. Consult a reliable published translation or the original Hindi text for literary analysis. Final historical assessment The evidence supports two related but distinct histories. Gilli-Danda is a widely distributed folk game with deep cultural roots but no securely established invention date. Cricket has a documented English history by the late sixteenth century and developed through written laws, clubs, professional play and colonial networks. Their shared striking action makes comparison meaningful, but direct descent from Gilli-Danda to cricket has not been proven. The game’s history is richer when this uncertainty is made explicit rather than hidden."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":1,"text":"MALLA YUDDHA TO MODERN WRESTLING History, Techniques, Akharas, Cultural Significance and the Evolution of Indian Wrestling A research report on the textual, material, social and sporting histories of South Asian grappling Research cut-off: 5 November 2026 Historical accuracy note: epic narratives, scholarly interpretations, visual evidence and modern sporting records are distinguished throughout. Uncertain claims are labelled rather than presented as fact."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":2,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 2 Contents and reading guide 01 | Scope and central argument 02 | Method and evidence 03 | Malla-yuddha as a term 04 | Kushti and pehlwani 05 | Epic narratives and caution 06 | Ramayana traditions 07 | Bhima and Jarasandha 08 | Krishna, Balarama and court wrestlers 09 | Hanuman and strength 10 | Fifth-century terracotta evidence 11 | What sculpture can tell us 12 | Manasollasa: context 13 | Mallavinoda and wrestler types 14 | Mallapurana: a specialised manual 15 | Jyeshthimalla community 16 | Arena and soil pit 17 | Royal patronage 18 | Persianate synthesis 19 | Mughal-era wrestling 20 | Seventeenth-century Malwa painting 21 | Raga Malava royal wrestlers 22 | Eighteenth-century exercise imagery 23 | Akhara as institution 24 | Guru, ustad and apprenticeship 25 | Physical preparation 26 | Diet and bodily discipline 27 | Ritual and everyday discipline 28 | Grappling principles 29 | Traditional bouts and local rules 30 | Women and historical visibility 31 | Colonial encounter 32 | Modern Olympic wrestling 33 | Freestyle and Greco-Roman 34 | India's early Olympic participation 35 | K. D. Jadhav and 1952 36 | Federation and national structure 37 | North Indian kushti 38 | Maharashtra and talim"},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":2,"text":"tyle and Greco-Roman 34 | India's early Olympic participation 35 | K. D. Jadhav and 1952 36 | Federation and national structure 37 | North Indian kushti 38 | Maharashtra and talim culture 39 | Gujarat and the Mallapurana 40 | Karnataka and the medieval Deccan 41 | Wrestling in visual culture 42 | Literature and ethnography 43 | Modernization of the training ground 44 | Athlete welfare 45 | International comparisons 46 | Preservation and living heritage 47 | Heritage status 48 | Research gaps 49 | Conclusion: continuity through change"},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":3,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 3 50 | Final historical assessment Terminology note: Malla-yuddha is used for Sanskrit textual and conceptual traditions; kushti/pehlwani for historically layered South Asian practices; and modern wrestling for codified international styles. The terms overlap but are not identical in every context."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":4,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 4 01 | Scope and central argument Malla-yuddha is commonly translated as wrestling combat: malla means wrestler and yuddha means battle or contest. The expression is important in Sanskrit literary and historical discussions, but it should not be treated as the title of one unchanging sport with one fixed rulebook. South Asian grappling varied by region, community, patron, period and purpose. Modern terms such as kushti and pehlwani are also historically layered and cannot simply be substituted for every ancient reference. This report argues for continuity through change. Epic narratives, medieval manuals, material culture, Persianate influences, akhara institutions, colonial-era sporting systems and international federations all contributed to wrestling in India. The evidence does not establish one inventor, exact birthplace or uninterrupted linear descent into Olympic wrestling. The report distinguishes fact, interpretation and legend. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":5,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 5 02 | Method and evidence Four evidence classes are used: literary texts, archaeological and visual objects, ethnography of living institutions, and modern sporting records. Each answers different questions. A mythological painting is not an eyewitness account of an actual bout; a modern akhara is not proof that every ancient training practice survived unchanged; and a sports database cannot reconstruct premodern social life. Museum metadata is used for object descriptions, scholarly work for interpretation, Sanskrit text studies for medieval knowledge, and official Olympic or federation sources for modern results and rules. Government summaries provide orientation, not specialist authority. Where dating is debated, the report states the uncertainty instead of selecting the most dramatic claim. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":6,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 6 03 | Malla-yuddha as a term The Sanskrit compound malla-yuddha conveys combat between wrestlers. Related words such as mallavidya, the knowledge or science of wrestling, and mallakala, the art of wrestling, occur in discussions of wrestling knowledge. These terms suggest that grappling could be understood as a learned discipline, not merely spontaneous physical struggle. Terminology changes across texts and regions. A Sanskrit author, Persianate court chronicler, village organiser and present-day athlete may use different labels for related activities. Historians should retain the source's own word and explain it. Translating all terms as wrestling is convenient, but can erase distinctions between combat, sport, professional practice and training culture. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":7,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 7 04 | Kushti and pehlwani Kushti is a Persian-derived word widely used for wrestling in South Asia. Pehlwan or pahalwan refers to a wrestler, while pehlwani can describe a broader discipline and way of life. These terms point to Persianate cultural influence, especially in historical worlds of courts, patronage and martial training. Joseph S. Alter's ethnography describes modern North Indian wrestling as a synthesis of indigenous and Persianate traditions, embedded in bodily discipline and social ethics. Pehlwani is not simply a new name pasted onto an unchanged ancient sport. The terms overlap with older malla-yuddha ideas but have distinct histories and social meanings. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":8,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 8 05 | Epic narratives and caution The Ramayana and Mahabharata preserve celebrated scenes of wrestling and close combat. These narratives show that wrestling-like contests were culturally intelligible and could represent strength, courage, honour and political legitimacy. They are foundational to the cultural memory of wrestling in India. Epic narratives developed over long periods and survive in multiple textual and regional forms. They are not automatically eyewitness records of the era in which a story is set. Their value lies in documenting literary and cultural representations of grappling. Exact rules, training schedules and institutions require additional evidence. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":9,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 9 06 | Ramayana traditions Ramayana traditions include scenes of powerful beings engaging in physical combat, and later interpretations connect the epic world with the prestige of wrestling. The language of combat may cover grappling alongside other forms of close fighting. Translators do not always map ancient terms neatly onto modern sporting categories. A responsible account should cite the specific recension, passage and translation. It should distinguish an epic episode from a historical report and from later devotional or performance interpretations. The Ramayana is evidence for the cultural significance of bodily contest, but does not by itself establish a modern akhara system or fixed technical curriculum. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":10,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 10 07 | Bhima and Jarasandha Bhima's struggle with Jarasandha is among the best-known wrestling narratives in the Mahabharata. The contest is presented as a test of extraordinary strength and endurance, and its outcome has consequences for political order. Wrestling becomes a dramatic means of resolving a conflict that ordinary weapons or negotiation cannot settle. The episode is valuable for understanding how grappling could symbolise power, courage and rightful victory. It should not be treated as a technical record of a historical bout. The narrative is concerned with heroic action and moral consequence, not the precise scoring conventions of a later competition. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":11,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 11 08 | Krishna, Balarama and court wrestlers The contest involving Krishna and Balarama against Chanura and Mushtika at Kamsa's court is an influential narrative. The wrestlers are presented as formidable opponents and the public contest becomes part of a political and moral confrontation. This story has been repeatedly represented in painting and performance. The National Museum, New Delhi, holds a mid-seventeenth-century Malwa painting titled Krishna and Balarama killing two wrestlers. That object is evidence for the visual reception of the story in the seventeenth century, not a documentary depiction of an ancient match. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":12,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 12 09 | Hanuman and strength Hanuman is revered in many wrestling settings as an exemplar of strength, devotion, service and self-control. His image or shrine may be present in an akhara, and wrestlers may invoke him before practice. This association is culturally significant, although ritual practice differs between institutions and communities. The ideal wrestler is often expected to combine strength with restraint and humility. Physical power is therefore not only an athletic quality but also a moral achievement. This helps explain why an akhara can function as a community and educational institution as well as a gym. Ritual should be documented locally, not assumed universal. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":13,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 13 10 | Fifth-century terracotta evidence The Los Angeles County Museum of Art catalogues a fifth-century earthenware panel titled Wrestlers, made in Uttar Pradesh, India, by an unknown maker. Its accession number is M.88.133.2. The panel shows two young wrestlers grappling, with a stand and heaps of treasure in the background, interpreted by the museum as a prize for the victor. This is a valuable early visual witness to wrestling as a recognisable contest. It does not reveal a complete ruleset, the names of techniques or whether the wrestlers belonged to an institution identical to a later akhara. Its strongest evidentiary value is that it documents the representation of wrestling in fifth-century India. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":14,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 14 11 | What sculpture can tell us A sculpture can preserve posture, body contact, clothing, spectatorship and symbolic details. It can show that an artist and audience recognised a particular activity. It cannot normally reveal the full rules of a match, the duration of training, the identity of participants or the exact name of a technique. Visual interpretation must consider artistic convention. Figures may be idealised, action compressed, and symbolic elements more important than literal detail. Captions should state title, date, medium, provenance, holding institution and catalogue number, then explain what is demonstrated and what remains unknown. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":15,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 15 12 | Manasollasa: context The Manasollasa, also known as Abhilashitarthachintamani, is an encyclopaedic Sanskrit work attributed to the Chalukya king Someshvara III and dated to about 1131 CE. It covers royal knowledge and life, including arts, leisure, animals, food and sports. Its inclusion of wrestling demonstrates that the subject belonged within courtly knowledge. The section commonly called Mallavinoda deals with wrestling entertainment or contests. Modern scholarship uses it to study wrestler classifications and preparation. The text is important, but it is not a modern coaching manual: some movements are named without detailed descriptions. Its historical value lies in its classification and courtly framing. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":16,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 16 13 | Mallavinoda and wrestler types Studies of the Manasollasa report categories of wrestlers such as Jyeshthika, Antarajyeshthika and Govala, with further distinctions by age or development. Such categories show that the text treated wrestlers as types whose bodies and capacities could be assessed. These classifications should be read as historical concepts, not modern physiological measurements. They reveal how a royal author or courtly tradition organised knowledge about strength, age and suitability for wrestling. They suggest formalised training and performance, but do not prove that every region used the same categories. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":17,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 17 14 | Mallapurana: a specialised manual The Mallapurana is a major Sanskrit source associated with the Jyeshthimalla professional wrestling community of Gujarat. The 1964 edition by B. J. Sandesara and R. N. Mehta describes it as a rare text on Indian wrestling, especially as practised by the Jyeshthimallas. It discusses wrestler types, physical attributes, training, exercises, techniques and arena preparation. The date and textual history require caution. Older scholarship often places the work around the thirteenth century, while more recent research discusses a fifteenth- or sixteenth-century setting or recension. This report treats it as medieval/early modern and flags the dating question for further philological research. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":18,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 18 15 | Jyeshthimalla community The Jyeshthimallas are important because the Mallapurana focuses on a specialised professional community rather than only legendary heroes. Its attention to wrestler types and specialised knowledge helps historians investigate occupation, status, training and the social organisation of physical expertise. This material cautions against describing all Indian wrestlers as one homogeneous group. Different communities and regions developed their own institutions and identities. Historical categories in the text should not be mapped directly onto present-day identities without specialist research. The text is a window into a particular social world, not a universal census. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":19,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 19 16 | Arena and soil pit The Mallapurana is discussed for its attention to the wrestling ground and preparation of the body. A suitable arena needed a surface fit for grappling and falling, cleared of hard or dangerous material. The ground was part of the technical environment, not merely a location. Descriptions of historical pits should not be converted into universal dimensions unless the source gives measurements. Local conditions, patronage and purpose shaped arenas. The continuing use of earth pits in many akharas is a visible continuity, but modern pits are maintained under contemporary conditions and cannot automatically be treated as unchanged medieval spaces. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":20,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 20 17 | Royal patronage Royal courts could support wrestlers as entertainers, athletes, retainers or symbols of strength. Wrestling offered a public demonstration of physical skill and could reinforce a ruler's prestige. Courtly texts and paintings provide evidence that contests were part of elite leisure and political spectacle. Patronage was only one part of wrestling's social history. Local contests, fairs, community networks and hereditary expertise also mattered. A history based only on royal courts would miss the ordinary institutions through which wrestlers trained and passed on knowledge. Court records, local histories, oral accounts and surviving akharas must be studied together. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":21,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 21 18 | Persianate synthesis Persianate vocabulary and wrestling culture interacted with indigenous South Asian practices through courts, migration, patronage and martial exchange. The terms kushti and pehlwani are visible traces of this contact. The resulting tradition is better understood as synthesis than simple replacement. Joseph Alter's work treats modern North Indian wrestling as culturally layered. Similarity between a technique and a move in another tradition does not prove a specific historical borrowing. Strong claims about transmission require dated manuals, teacher lineages, court accounts or other corroborating evidence. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":22,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 22 19 | Mughal-era wrestling Mughal and other Indo-Persian court cultures valued physical contests and displays of strength. Court chronicles and visual sources can illuminate wrestling's prestige, but must be read according to genre. A chronicle may praise a patron; a painting may idealise bodies; neither necessarily records the daily practice of ordinary wrestlers. The Mughal period is best treated as a phase of interaction and transformation. Persianate vocabulary and courtly sponsorship met regional traditions. The precise movement of techniques and institutions remains a subject for research. Avoid claims that one dynasty invented all later kushti or that every modern practice descends directly from a single court. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":23,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 23 20 | Seventeenth-century Malwa painting The National Museum, New Delhi, records a mid-seventeenth-century Malwa painting titled Krishna and Balarama killing two wrestlers, accession 49.19/50. It is on paper, uses natural colour, and has an unknown artist. The catalogue identifies Malwa in Central India as its origin and find place. The image is a narrative representation of an epic/Puranic episode. It provides evidence for how wrestlers and wrestling stories were visualised in seventeenth-century Central India. It is not an eyewitness record of a historical match. Catalogue metadata should accompany any reproduction. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":24,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 24 21 | Raga Malava royal wrestlers The Metropolitan Museum of Art catalogue lists Raga Malava: Royal Wrestlers, dated about 1680 and attributed to India, Basohli or Nurpur. It is an opaque-watercolour painting on paper, object L.2022.30.5, associated with the Howard Hodgkin Indian Collection Trust and photographed by the Ashmolean Museum, Oxford. The painting belongs to a raga-related artistic context. Its value is not limited to documenting sport: it shows wrestling as part of courtly visual and musical culture. It can help researchers study posture, costume and artistic conventions, but cannot establish precise rules or training systems. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":25,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 25 22 | Eighteenth-century exercise imagery An art-market catalogue describes an eighteenth-century Bikaner/Jaipur-school painting of exercising wrestlers, including a pole exercise, Indian clubs (mugdals) and a round stone weight. Because this is a commercial rather than major public-museum record, its attribution and provenance should be independently verified before academic use. The image is consistent with a broader history of wrestlers using exercises beyond live grappling. Clubs and weights develop strength and coordination, while pole and bodyweight exercises challenge balance and control. Treat it as a research lead with a caveat, not definitive proof of a standard national training routine. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":26,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 26 23 | Akhara as institution An akhara is often translated as a wrestling gymnasium, but it can also be a social institution. It may organise instruction, ritual, discipline, mentorship and community identity. The guru or ustad transmits technical knowledge and may also teach conduct, etiquette and the responsibilities of senior practitioners. Joseph Alter's ethnography, The Wrestler's Body (1992), is a key study of North Indian pehlwani as a way of life. It documents how physical training connects with moral ideas and social relationships. It is a detailed study of particular settings, not a universal account of every akhara. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":27,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 27 24 | Guru, ustad and apprenticeship Wrestling knowledge is often transmitted through demonstration, repetition, correction and sparring. A teacher observes posture, grip, balance and timing, then adjusts a student's movement. Senior wrestlers may help juniors learn the etiquette of practice and the unwritten expectations of the institution. This apprenticeship model creates a chain of transmission. It can preserve local terminology and technique absent from published manuals. Oral history is essential: interviews with retired wrestlers, teachers and families can reveal how a specific akhara changed over decades. Testimony should be dated, attributed and cross-checked. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":28,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 28 25 | Physical preparation Traditional wrestling preparation can combine running, squats, push-up variations, partner exercises, club swinging, rope work, pole exercises and live grappling. The aims include strength, endurance, grip, mobility, coordination and the ability to apply force while resisting an opponent. Exact routines differ across akharas and periods. Medieval texts name exercises but do not always describe them sufficiently to reconstruct every movement. Modern training may add periodised strength programmes, conditioning, video analysis and recovery monitoring. The continuity is systematic preparation; exact exercises, volume and sequencing have changed. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":29,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 29 26 | Diet and bodily discipline Food is a major theme in wrestling culture. Historical sources such as the Manasollasa and Mallapurana are discussed for attention to diet and preparation. Modern akhara narratives often mention milk, ghee, almonds, pulses and substantial meals, but actual diets vary by region, resources, religion and coaching philosophy. Alter's ethnography shows that food is embedded in a moral economy of the body. Eating, sleeping, restraint and exercise can be organised around becoming a wrestler. Traditional foodways are historically significant, but should not be presented as universal medical prescriptions. Modern athlete nutrition requires evidence-based advice. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":30,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 30 27 | Ritual and everyday discipline Some akharas include practices such as saluting the earth, honouring the guru, invoking Hanuman or maintaining a clean pit. These actions can express humility, gratitude and respect for the arena. Their form varies, and researchers should document what a particular institution actually does. Ritual helps explain why wrestling may be experienced as more than exercise. It connects the athlete to teachers, training partners, community and inherited ideals. The ethical dimension can be valuable, but should not be romanticised: discipline must coexist with safety, consent, safeguarding and medical care. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":31,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 31 28 | Grappling principles Historical manuals and living traditions refer to techniques, but names and descriptions are not always stable across time and language. A modern list of named throws should not be projected onto medieval texts unless the source supports the identification. Broad principles include posture control, breaking balance, securing grips, entering, turning or lifting and seeking a decisive position. These principles occur across many grappling cultures, but permitted actions differ. Traditional bouts may follow local conventions; freestyle allows leg attacks; Greco-Roman prohibits holds below the waist and active leg use to execute actions. Technique must be understood within the rules and purpose of a contest. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":32,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 32 29 | Traditional bouts and local rules Traditional wrestling events may be organised at fairs, festivals, village gatherings or by patrons. A bout can test strength, reputation and skill, and may be governed by local conventions rather than one national rulebook. Victory may depend on a fall or a locally recognised condition of defeat. Regional variation is part of the tradition. Researchers should ask who set the rules, what counted as a decisive win, whether weight or age was considered, and how disputes were settled. Oral accounts and local event records are crucial for reconstructing these details. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":33,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 33 30 | Women and historical visibility Women's participation in historical Indian wrestling requires careful research. A male-centred source does not prove that women never practised grappling or strength training, but researchers should not invent ancient female lineages without evidence. The record is uneven, and social restrictions affected who could train publicly and whose activities were recorded. Modern women's wrestling has grown within changing social and institutional conditions. Access to coaching, facilities, family support, school sport and competition pathways has been crucial. Indian women wrestlers have challenged assumptions about gender and strength. A complete history must include them while distinguishing documented developments from uncertain ancient claims. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":34,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 34 31 | Colonial encounter Colonial rule changed the environment in which Indian physical practices were described, organised and promoted. Educational institutions, public spectacles, print culture and sporting regulations influenced how wrestling was classified. Indian wrestlers and patrons adapted to these changes while maintaining local institutions. Some nationalist writing presented wrestling as an indigenous source of strength and self-improvement. Such narratives are historically important but can simplify the past by describing modern practice as unchanged ancient inheritance. A layered account recognises local continuity alongside Persianate influence, colonial encounter and international codification. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":35,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 35 32 | Modern Olympic wrestling United World Wrestling records Greco-Roman wrestling in the modern Olympic programme at Athens 1896 and freestyle at St. Louis 1904. Wrestling was part of the ancient Olympic Games at Olympia from 708 BCE, according to UWW. Ancient and modern Olympic traditions are related in name and ideal, but their rules and institutions differ. International codification introduced common rules, weight categories, time limits, officiating and governing structures. These features made cross-national competition possible. Indian wrestlers entering international events had to adapt to a new sporting environment, even when earlier experience came from local wrestling and akhara practice. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":36,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 36 33 | Freestyle and Greco-Roman United World Wrestling explains that freestyle permits holds and attacks involving the legs, including leg grabs and trips. Greco-Roman restricts holds below the belt and prohibits using the legs actively to perform an action. Both styles aim to control and expose the opponent and can end with a fall or points decision. These rules produce different tactical systems. Freestyle includes leg attacks and defence against them; Greco-Roman emphasises upper-body ties, throws and lifts. A wrestler moving from kushti to international sport must learn new rules, mat boundaries, scoring and match management. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":37,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 37 34 | India's early Olympic participation Olympedia lists Indian wrestling participation at the 1920 Antwerp Games, including Dinkarrao Shinde and Kumar Navale. Early international participation took place under colonial conditions, with limited resources and developing sporting institutions. Result lists provide a starting point for tracing athletes and events. Participation itself is historically significant. It shows Indian wrestlers entering international competition and negotiating the demands of a codified sport. A full account should connect results with regional training institutions, selection processes, travel conditions, patronage and athletes who may be poorly represented in national narratives. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":38,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 38 35 | K. D. Jadhav and 1952 Khashaba Dadasaheb Jadhav won bronze in men's freestyle wrestling at the 1952 Helsinki Olympics. The Indian Olympic Association records his medal in the under-57 kg category and identifies it as India's first individual Olympic medal as an independent nation. Olympedia records his sixth-place finish at London 1948 and bronze in 1952. Jadhav's achievement links local wrestling culture in Maharashtra with international competition. His story became a landmark in Indian sport and made wrestling a source of national pride. Olympic databases establish results, but regional archives, newspapers and oral histories are needed to reconstruct training, financial barriers and support systems. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":39,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 39 36 | Federation and national structure United World Wrestling lists the Wrestling Federation of India as affiliated since 1961 and recognises freestyle, Greco-Roman, women's wrestling and beach wrestling among its styles. Affiliation connected Indian competition with international governance, continental events and world championships. Federations provide selection, rules, coaching structures and international representation, but the grassroots base depends on local clubs, akharas, schools and state associations. A strong national system needs transparent governance, athlete welfare, accessible facilities and pathways from local competition to elite sport. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":40,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 40 37 | North Indian kushti North Indian kushti is strongly associated with the akhara, earth pit and disciplined training culture. Punjab, Haryana, Uttar Pradesh and Rajasthan have prominent wrestling networks, although the history and institutions of each region differ. Public dangals may attract crowds and connect wrestling with fairs, festivals and local prestige. Some athletes train for both mud-pit kushti and mat wrestling; others specialise. The two environments overlap but are not identical. Research should identify the specific district, institution, teacher and time period rather than assume one uniform North Indian style. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":41,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 41 38 | Maharashtra and talim culture Maharashtra has a strong tradition of talim and akhara institutions, local dangals and rural wrestling culture. K. D. Jadhav's Olympic bronze gives the state a prominent place in modern Indian wrestling history. Local histories connect wrestling with neighbourhood identity, patronage, physical education and community prestige. The word talim is used for training institutions in Maharashtra and other regions. A talim's history may be preserved in municipal records, local newspapers, family collections and oral testimony. Statewide generalisations should be supplemented by local histories of Kolhapur, Pune, Satara and Sangli. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":42,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 42 39 | Gujarat and the Mallapurana Gujarat is central to the textual history of Indian wrestling because of the Mallapurana and its focus on the Jyeshthimalla professional community. The text offers rare evidence about wrestler classification, physical qualities, exercise and the social organisation of specialist wrestling knowledge. The medieval categories should not be mapped directly onto contemporary communities without evidence. Research should pair the Sanskrit text with regional social history, manuscript study and oral testimony. Gujarat's importance lies in preserving an unusually rich textual record, not proving one origin for all Indian wrestling. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":43,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 43 40 | Karnataka and the medieval Deccan The Manasollasa was composed under the Chalukya king Someshvara III and offers a medieval Deccan perspective on wrestling. Its Mallavinoda section shows that wrestling belonged to courtly knowledge and leisure. It is a rare source for how wrestlers were classified and their preparation understood. Present-day Karnataka wrestling traditions have their own histories. Continuity from the medieval text to a modern institution must be demonstrated through records, not assumed from geography alone. Comparative research across inscriptions, manuscripts, local histories and oral accounts could clarify regional developments. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":44,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 44 41 | Wrestling in visual culture Wrestling appears in visual culture as mythological combat, courtly entertainment, athletic exercise and public spectacle. The fifth-century LACMA terracotta, the seventeenth-century National Museum painting and the Met's late seventeenth-century royal wrestlers represent different genres and contexts. Images can help study posture, costume, spectatorship and the cultural status of wrestlers. Artists may idealise bodies or use symbolic conventions. Every image should carry a caption with date, medium, provenance, institution and a note explaining what it demonstrates. Rights and reproduction conditions must be checked. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":45,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 45 42 | Literature and ethnography Beyond epics, wrestling is discussed in Sanskrit technical literature, courtly encyclopaedias, regional histories, newspapers, biographies and modern ethnography. The Manasollasa and Mallapurana are important for wrestling knowledge; Joseph Alter's 1992 ethnography explains the lived culture of North Indian pehlwani. Literature and ethnography reveal different things. A poem or novel may show how strength and masculinity were imagined; a manual may prescribe practice; an ethnography records a particular community. A good history uses these sources without treating them as interchangeable. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":46,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 46 43 | Modernization of the training ground Traditional kushti often uses an earth pit; international wrestling uses a standardised mat. Surfaces change grip, falling, movement and the organisation of practice. Modern training may add strength equipment, video analysis, sports science, physiotherapy and recovery monitoring, while some athletes continue traditional exercises. Modernization is not simply replacement. Many institutions combine inherited routines with contemporary methods. The key historical questions are what changed, who introduced change, and how wrestlers adapted. Local case studies are more informative than broad claims that traditional wrestling either disappeared or remained untouched. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":47,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 47 44 | Athlete welfare Contemporary wrestling requires attention to injury prevention, safe weight management, hydration, recovery, safeguarding and medical support. Traditional emphasis on discipline can be valuable, but it must not justify coercive training or preventable harm. Young athletes need age-appropriate instruction and protection. Sports science can complement cultural knowledge. Coaches can preserve traditional exercises while evaluating load, technique and recovery. Historical admiration for toughness should not be confused with a modern health recommendation. Claims about diet, supplements or weight cutting require current medical evidence. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":48,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 48 45 | International comparisons Many societies developed grappling traditions, including ancient Greek wrestling, Persian wrestling, Central Asian styles, Japanese sumo and regional folk wrestling. Shared features such as gripping, balance-breaking and throwing do not prove a common origin. Similar techniques can emerge independently because bodies and mechanics create recurring solutions. Comparison should focus on rules, clothing, surface, permitted holds, victory conditions, training institutions and social role. Claims that one national tradition invented all others are unsupported. Historical interaction can be demonstrated through migration, trade, courts, military exchange and modern sporting networks. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":49,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 49 46 | Preservation and living heritage Preserving wrestling requires more than occasional demonstrations. It needs viable akharas, trained teachers, safe facilities, access for children and women, local documentation and pathways that allow athletes to move between traditional and modern competition. Oral history can preserve memories of senior wrestlers. Preservation should not freeze a living tradition into a museum display. Communities should decide what to retain, adapt or discontinue. Digital archives, school partnerships, local dangals and coach education can help when they accurately credit practitioners and respect the institutions that hold the knowledge. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":50,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 50 47 | Heritage status Traditional wrestling is discussed in cultural heritage contexts, but formal UNESCO or government recognition must be checked against official listings. This research did not establish a standalone UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage inscription for Indian malla-yuddha, kushti or pehlwani. It would be incorrect to claim such a status without an authoritative listing. Formal designation is not the only measure of value. A practice can be culturally important without appearing on an international list. Local recognition, living institutions, oral transmission and community participation are significant forms of continuity. Researchers should distinguish official designation from academic attention and grassroots preservation. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":51,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 51 48 | Research gaps Important questions remain open. The dating and transmission of the Mallapurana require close philological work; the relationship between its prescriptions and later living practice needs regionally grounded comparison; and the everyday history of women's participation deserves deeper archival research. Further work should combine Sanskrit and Persian textual scholarship, archaeology, art history, oral history, ethnography and sports science. Longitudinal studies of specific akharas could trace teacher lineages, changes in training, athlete migration and movement between mud-pit kushti and international mat wrestling. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":52,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 52 49 | Conclusion: continuity through change Malla-yuddha is an important term and concept in South Asian wrestling history, but Indian wrestling is broader than a single ancient label. Epic narratives preserve memorable contests; medieval texts systematise aspects of wrestling knowledge; Persianate influences shape vocabulary and practice; akharas sustain training cultures; and modern federations connect Indian athletes to international competition. The transition to modern wrestling is best understood as adaptation, not simple replacement. Earth-pit traditions continue alongside mat sport; local identities coexist with national and Olympic pathways; and ideals of discipline, strength and community remain important as rules, training science and athlete welfare evolve. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":53,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 53 50 | Final historical assessment The evidence supports several conclusions: wrestling was represented in Indian literature and art over many centuries; a fifth-century terracotta panel depicts wrestlers grappling; medieval Sanskrit works discuss wrestler classifications, training and arenas; Persianate vocabulary and practices contributed to later kushti and pehlwani; and modern Indian wrestlers became part of international competition during the twentieth century. The evidence does not establish a single inventor, exact origin date, uniform ancient rules, or an unbroken direct line from epic malla-yuddha to Olympic freestyle or Greco-Roman wrestling. The distinction between fact, interpretation and legend is not a weakness. It is the basis of a credible history. Evidence note: Interpret this section according to its source type. Literary narratives, visual objects, ethnographic observations and modern sporting records answer different historical questions."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":54,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 54 Comparative table: traditional kushti and modern wrestling Dimension Traditional akhara / kushti contexts Modern international wrestling Surface Prepared earth or mud pit in many akharas; local variation Standardised wrestling mat Rules May vary by locality, event and tradition Codified rules and officiating Victory Local conventions; often a decisive fall Fall, points, technical superiority or rule-defined outcome Training Grappling, bodyweight work, clubs and partner drills Technical practice, strength/conditioning, analysis and recovery Institution Akhara or talim; guru/ustad and community network Clubs, academies, federations and national teams Competition Dangal, local contest, fair or festival Weight classes, timed bouts and international rankings Knowledge Apprenticeship, oral instruction and local custom Coaching systems, written rules and sport science This is a broad comparison, not a claim that every traditional institution follows identical practice. Some modern athletes train in both environments."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":55,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 55 Modern Olympic styles: technical distinction Feature Freestyle Greco-Roman Leg attacks Permitted Not permitted Tripping / active leg use Permitted within rules Prohibited Grips below waist Permitted within rules Prohibited Olympic introduction 1904 1896 Based on United World Wrestling descriptions of Olympic styles. Regulations can change; competitors should consult the current rulebook for event-specific details."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":56,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 56 Concept diagram: the akhara as a training ecosystem Guru / Ustad — Instruction, correction, conduct ↓ Daily preparation — Strength, mobility, conditioning, recovery ↓ Technical practice — Grip, balance, entries, throws, defence ↓ Live wrestling — Partner practice, sparring, local bouts ↓ Community and identity — Mentorship, ritual, belonging, transmission ↓ Figure 1. Original conceptual diagram for this report. It is explanatory, not a historical illustration or universal organisational chart."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":57,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 57 Appendix: selected historical visual records Wrestlers Fifth century CE; Uttar Pradesh, India; earthenware; unknown maker; LACMA accession M.88.133.2. Two young wrestlers grapple; the museum notes a victor's reward in the background. Strong early visual evidence for wrestling representation. Catalogue: https://collections.lacma.org/object/61054 Krishna and Balarama killing two wrestlers Mid-17th century CE; Malwa, Central India; paper and natural colour; unknown artist; National Museum, New Delhi; accession 49.19/50. Mythological narrative painting showing the visual reception of wrestling stories in seventeenth-century Central India. Catalogue: https://museumsofindia.gov.in/repository/record/nat_del-49-19-50-26679 Raga Malava: Royal Wrestlers c. 1680; India, Basohli or Nurpur; opaque watercolour on paper; Met object L.2022.30.5; photo © Ashmolean Museum, Oxford. Courtly/raga-series visual representation; useful for art history, not a rules manual. Catalogue: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/825652 Indian Jaipur School Painting of Exercising Wrestlers Attributed to Bikaner, c. 1760; commercial catalogue description; provenance and reproduction rights require independent"},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":57,"text":"5652 Indian Jaipur School Painting of Exercising Wrestlers Attributed to Bikaner, c. 1760; commercial catalogue description; provenance and reproduction rights require independent verification. Depicts exercise clubs, a pole exercise and a stone weight. Treat as a research lead, not a definitive institutional record. Catalogue: https://www.michaelbackmanltd.com/object/indian-jaipur-school-miniature-painting/"},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":58,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 58 Appendix: glossary Akhara A training space or gymnasium; often also a social and instructional institution. Dangal A wrestling contest or event; meaning and format vary by region. Guru / Ustad Teacher or master who transmits technical knowledge and, in many settings, conduct. Kushti Persian-derived term widely used for wrestling in South Asia. Malla Wrestler; a term found in Sanskrit sources. Malla-yuddha Wrestling combat; Sanskrit expression used in literary and historical discussions. Mallavinoda Section of the Manasollasa concerned with wrestling contests or entertainment. Mallapurana Sanskrit text associated with the Jyeshthimalla community and wrestling knowledge. Pehlwan / Pahalwan Wrestler; term associated with Persianate vocabulary and pehlwani culture. Pehlwani South Asian wrestling discipline and associated way of life, especially in North Indian contexts. Talim Training institution or gymnasium, a term used in Maharashtra and elsewhere."},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":59,"text":"Malla Yuddha to Modern Wrestling Page 59 Bibliography and research sources Primary texts, editions and material records • B. J. Sandesara and R. N. Mehta (eds.), Mallapurana: A Rare Sanskrit Text on Indian Wrestling Especially as Practised by the Jyeshthimallas. Gaekwad's Oriental Series 144. Baroda: Oriental Institute, 1964. https://ci.nii.ac.jp/ncid/BA16607702 • Someshvara III, Manasollasa / Abhilashitarthachintamani, c. 1131 CE. Textual orientation: https://indike.org/pramana/in.art.manasollasa • Los Angeles County Museum of Art, Wrestlers, fifth century, object M.88.133.2. https://collections.lacma.org/object/61054 • National Museum, New Delhi, Krishna and Balarama killing two wrestlers, accession 49.19/50. https://museumsofindia.gov.in/repository/record/nat_del-49-19-50-26679 • The Metropolitan Museum of Art, Raga Malava: Royal Wrestlers, c. 1680, object L.2022.30.5. https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/825652 Scholarly research • Joseph S. Alter, The Wrestler's Body: Identity and Ideology in North India. Berkeley: University of California Press, 1992. https://publishing.cdlib.org/ucpressebooks/public/book/the-wrestler-s-body-identity-and-ideology-in-north-india.html • Ajay Jacob Thomas, From Mallapurana to The"},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":59,"text":"rnia Press, 1992. https://publishing.cdlib.org/ucpressebooks/public/book/the-wrestler-s-body-identity-and-ideology-in-north-india.html • Ajay Jacob Thomas, From Mallapurana to The Science of Wrestling. Volume I: Changing Facets of Indian Wrestling Manuals, 2025. https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/03769836251334007 • Jerome Armstrong, Uncovering Vyayama in Yoga, Journal of Yoga Studies (2023). https://journalofyogastudies.org/index.php/JoYS/article/download/JoYS.2023.V4.07/26/ • Jagdish Parshad, Wrestling in Indian Arts, Academic Discourse 10, no. 1 (2021), pp. 44-50. https://addtest.indianjournals.com/article/adi-10-1-007 Institutional and modern sport sources • United World Wrestling, The Sport, Olympic styles and history. https://uww.org/about-uww/sport • United World Wrestling, Disciplines, descriptions of Greco-Roman and freestyle. https://uww.org/disciplines • United World Wrestling, Wrestling Federation of India, affiliation and styles. https://uww.org/about-uww/national-federations/wrestling-federation-india • Indian Olympic Association, Helsinki 1952, Khashaba Jadhav's bronze medal. https://olympic.ind.in/international-game/summer-olympics-games-helsinki-finland-1952/ • Olympedia, Khashaba Jadhav, Olympic"},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":59,"text":"iation, Helsinki 1952, Khashaba Jadhav's bronze medal. https://olympic.ind.in/international-game/summer-olympics-games-helsinki-finland-1952/ • Olympedia, Khashaba Jadhav, Olympic results. https://www.olympedia.org/athletes/58694 • Olympedia, India in Wrestling, historical Indian Olympic participation. https://www.olympedia.org/countries/IND/sports/WRE.1 • Press Information Bureau, Government of India, overview of Indian wrestling and malla-yuddha. https://www.pib.gov.in/FeaturesDeatils.aspx?ModuleId+=+2&NoteId;=153305\u0000=1®=3 • CBSE Academic, Martial Arts Traditions, discussion of Mallapurana, Manasollasa and historical training spaces. https://cbseacademic.nic.in/web_material/doc/ktpi/9_Martial_Arts_old.pdf Limitations and final note This report is a structured research synthesis, not a critical edition or exhaustive archival history. It supports a strong account of textual traditions, selected visual evidence, akhara ethnography and modern international sport. It does not establish a single inventor, exact origin year, universal ancient rules or an uninterrupted direct line from epic malla-yuddha to Olympic wrestling. The Mallapurana's dating and transmission deserve further philological work. Museum images are linked to"},{"game":"wrestling","title":"04 · Malla-Yuddha to Modern Wrestling","file":"04-malla-yuddha-to-wrestling.pdf","page":59,"text":"an uninterrupted direct line from epic malla-yuddha to Olympic wrestling. The Mallapurana's dating and transmission deserve further philological work. Museum images are linked to catalogue records rather than embedded because reproduction permissions vary. For formal academic submission, verify quotations against full editions and obtain image permissions before reproduction."}];
